    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Assistant-Professor_JR0000080487-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). McGill University Department of Economics Faculty of Arts Assistant Professor, Any Field – Department of Economics Subfield: Economic Theory The Department of Economics at McGill University invites applications for a tenure-track position in Economic Theory. The appointment is expected to be at the rank of Assistant Professor. The position start date is August 1, 2027. For more information about the Department of Economics please see: www.mcgill.ca/economics/ .  Inquiries may be directed to the Chair of the Department of Economics, Professor Fabian Lange, at fabian.lange@mcgill.ca . Job Duties: Teaching, research, and service to the institution.  All McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. Qualifications and Education Requirements: Candidates should have completed their PhD or be near completion. An applicant’s record of performance must provide evidence of outstanding research potential and the ability/potential to teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate level, as well as evidence (or intentions) of undergraduate and graduate supervision and of mentoring students with diverse lived experiences. Faculty/Department/Unit: Department of Economics Faculty of Arts Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Application Deadline: Applications should be received by November 15, 2026 , to guarantee full consideration, although applications will continue to be reviewed until the position is filled. How to Apply: Applications must be submitted online at: https://econjobmarket.org/positions/12650 . Candidates selected for interview will be expected to submit the same information to the McGill local Human Resources platform on Workday. Please refer to the Applying at McGill University – Guide for Candidates (External or Former Employees) job aid for instructions on how to apply in Workday. Required Documents: The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter and curriculum vitae; Recent research papers; Three confidential letters of reference submitted directly by the referees. Depending on your nationality, it may be necessary for you to have a valid Canadian Visa to travel should you be invited to a campus visit. Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada (or apply and obtain authorization) and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. However, knowledge of French is an asset. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Royal-Victoria-Hosp/CHIEF-OF-THE-DEPARTMENT-OF-OBSTETRICS-AND-GYNECOLOGY--MCGILL-UNIVERSITY-HEALTH-CENTRE-AND-CHAIR-OF-THE-MCGILL-UNIVERSITY-DEPARTMENT-OF-OBSTETRICS-AND-GYNECOLOGY--FACULTY-OF-MEDICINE-AND-HEALTH-SCIENCES_JR0000080568": {
      "title": "CHIEF OF THE DEPARTMENT OF OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY, MCGILL UNIVERSITY HEALTH CENTRE AND CHAIR OF THE MCGILL UNIVERSITY DEPARTMENT OF OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY, FACULTY OF MEDICINE AND HEALTH SCIENCES",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-14\nPart time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The McGill University Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences (FMHS) and the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC) invite applications for a full-time position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor as Chief of the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, McGill University Health Centre and Chair of the McGill University Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology. A tenure stream position may be available for the successful candidate. Located in the heart of Montreal, McGill counts itself among the finest research-intensive universities in the world. With a strong focus on interdisciplinary learning and celebrated for its rigorous intellectual pursuits and commitment to nurturing a diverse community of scholars, students, and staff, McGill University is a hub for intellectual curiosity, creativity, and growth. The FMHS is comprised of over 5000 academic faculty, 970 staff members, 8,000 students, residents, and fellows, across six schools and two campuses. The FMHS research and teaching missions are conducted in collaboration with multiple teaching hospitals and health authority partners across Quebec. The Faculty is committed to uniting science and humanism to train outstanding health professionals and scientists who together lead the discovery, innovation, and care needed to improve the health of Quebeckers and communities around the world. The MUHC is an adult and paediatric academic health centre affiliated with McGill’s Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences with an international reputation for excellence in clinical care, research and education. It has a responsibility for tertiary paediatric and adult services for the University Integrated Health and Social Services Network of McGill ( Réseau universitaire intégré de santé et de services sociaux McGill ) across a vast territory of Québec in addition to providing comprehensive healthcare services to its local community. Patients receive care in English and French, and through interpretative services, a host of other languages as may be necessary to support the multicultural population it serves. The MUHC’s vision is to make a local to global impact by mobilizing together on sustainable, innovative practices, research, and teaching that deliver compassionate world-class complex care to people of all ages and promote a healthier planet. The McGill Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology is internationally recognized for its academic excellence, clinical leadership, and innovation in women’s health. Comprised of an interdisciplinary community of physicians, scientists, trainees, and support staff, the Department’s mission is to pursue excellence in comprehensive, patient-centered care, exceptional education, and the translation of knowledge, therapeutics, and technology. Our vibrant academic ecosystem encompasses approximately 100 clinical and research faculty members distributed across specialized divisions of Obstetrics, Maternal-Fetal Medicine, Gynecology, Reproductive Endocrinology and Infertility, Gynecological Oncology, Urogynecology, and Basic Science Research. Operating across McGill’s premier teaching hospitals and research institutes, the Department boasts a distinguished track record of clinical excellence, innovation, education, and translational research across McGill’s teaching hospitals and research institutes, ultimately driving meaningful advancements in women’s health outcomes locally, nationally, and globally. Responsibilities as Chair of the McGill University Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology We are seeking a respected academic physician with demonstrated leadership in clinical care, education, research, and administration to lead one of Canada’s premier academic departments in women’s health. The successful candidate will be expected to provide strategic and operational leadership for the Department’s academic, teaching, and research missions throughout the McGill network. As Chair of the McGill University Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, the successful candidate will be expected to strengthen the Department’s teaching and research environments through strategic recruitment, mentorship, and faculty career development, support educational leaders in postgraduate and undergraduate medicine and research-based graduate studies, and strengthen collaborations within the FMHS and across the University and its affiliated health network. As a leader within McGill’s ecosystem, the Chair will champion women’s health and will seek opportunities for synergy across sites and activities, while supporting established and encouraging emerging areas of excellence. The successful candidate is an energetic and thoughtful leader with demonstrated abilities to manage change, encourage excellence in complex, high-stakes environments and able to foster a sense of belonging and shared purpose within the organization. Applicants for this position should have substantive experience in both research and teaching at a high level. Applicants should have experience in administration and leadership, with the ability to build effective teams, mentor colleagues, staff, and students, and engage with clinical and community partners, academic institutions, and governmental agencies. The successful candidate is responsible for the academic leadership (i.e., academic, clinical, educational, and research activities) and advancement of the Department across its various subspecialties and programs, including all McGill teaching hospitals and affiliated research institutes. Responsibilities include: Assume strategic, operational, academic, and financial responsibilities for the McGill University Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology; Provide effective mentoring and guidance on academic appointments, promotion, and tenure and faculty development; Assure inclusive internal governance to effectively coordinate the variety of activities, sites, and priorities of the Department; Collaborate with Program Directors on education and training matters Build partnerships with sister units sharing common interests; Promote excellence in research, teaching, and clinical care; Represent the Department in Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences fora, including the Faculty Leadership Council and the School of Medicine Clinical Leadership Council; Oversee undergraduate and postgraduate medical education programs in collaboration with the Associate Deans and program directors; Work collaboratively with hospital research institute directors to strengthen innovation and research; Coordinate academic planning and recruitment across the McGill RUISSS network (Réseau universitaire intégré de santé et de services sociaux) and affiliated hospitals to foster successful academic programs through partnerships and complementarity, ensuring hospital recruitments outside the MUHC carry the formal recommendation of the hiring institution's Obstetrician and Gynecologist-in-Chief; Serve as a dedicated advocate for faculty and staff resources, driving administrative and research support initiatives across University, MUHC, and Santé Québec leadership. Responsibilities as Chief of the Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, MUHC The Chief is responsible for the quality, organization, and strategic planning of clinical services at the MUHC. Responsibilities include: Ensuring high-quality patient care and alignment of departmental clinical activities with institutional priorities; Providing leadership in the development and integration of clinical programs across MUHC sites; Ensuring each member of the MUHC Department has clearly defined clinical and academic responsibilities, reviewed at least once every two years; Working collaboratively with MUHC leadership and other department chiefs; Reporting to the Associate Director of Medical and Professional Services regarding clinical organization, resource allocation, and departmental performance; Reports to the Chair of the Council of Physicians, Dentists, Pharmacists and Midwives (CPDPMW) of the MUHC to ensure high-quality patient care; Supporting strategic planning and fostering integration between clinical and academic missions across McGill teaching hospitals; Advance inter-institutional alignment and strategic synergy across the affiliated teaching hospitals, leveraging each site’s distinct operational strengths to optimize their collective academic, clinical, and research missions. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates should ideally: Hold a medical degree with specialist certification in Obstetrics and Gynecology; Be eligible for licensure in the Province of Quebec; Have demonstrated leadership abilities, a proven track-record in research and teaching, and the skills and experience needed to lead diverse teams in a collaborative, collegial manner; Have a record of success in managing complex units and multi-site clinical services, and the experience in navigating institutional, academic, and public policy channels to make progress on multifaceted issues ; Be an effective proponent of integrated research, exceptional clinical care, and innovative, inclusive teaching; Show a strong commitment to undergraduate and postgraduate medical education; Have a record of research excellence linked seamlessly with excellence in clinical care and a clear understanding of research with clinical-translational impact; Demonstrate excellence in organization, interdisciplinary collaboration, network development, and human resource management; Have a track record of effective and synergistic collaborations; Knowledge of the Quebec healthcare system will be considered an asset; Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families, and health care professionals. The successful candidate will also be expected to be actively involved in all aspects of McGill’s academic mission (i.e., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees). McGill faculty members are also expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion is also expected. The following supporting documents are required: CV and cover letter describing leadership experience and vision; Statement of research and educational contributions (combine all documents in a single PDF); The names and contact information of three referees. Candidates should submit their application within 30 days of publication of this advertisement. Applications must be submitted online via the McGill Careers Site. For any questions regarding the recruitment process, please contact the primary recruiter at   liliana.ospina@mcgill.ca   or acadsec.med@mcgill.ca .  McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbal and written. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/JGH---Jewish-General-Hosp/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Medicine--Division-of-Physical-Medicine-and-Rehabilitation--C3-260911-_JR0000080632": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical), Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (C3-260911)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Medicine in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position in the Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical). The successful candidate will be based at Santé Québec Centre-Ouest-de-l'Île-de-Montréal – Universitaire, with primary clinical responsibilities at the Constance-Lethbridge site of the Lethbridge-Layton-Mackay (LLM) Rehabilitation Centre. The professorial rank will be determined according to the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. This is a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. The Department of Medicine is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals.  Constance Lethbridge is one of McGill’s major rehabilitation teaching sites affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec. The teams at LLM are dedicated to working with clients to fully maximize their autonomy and participation in the community through specialized and ultra-specialized rehabilitation services to adults living with physical disabilities across a broad range of neurological, musculoskeletal, and complex rehabilitation conditions while also supporting their families.   Job Duties Please see the Department of Medicine (DOM) website for McGill DOM Career path definitions. Our career path definitions specify typical ranges of time allotted to clinical work, teaching (clinical and didactic), research and administration. As a Clinician-Teacher, the successful candidate’s major non-clinical focus will be on teaching the next generation of physicians. Teaching activity will include both didactic and clinical teaching. As a Clinician-Educator, you will focus on education research of sufficient caliber to publish in peer-reviewed journals or education administration with a focus on leading/developing world-class training programs. The successful candidate will be assigned to the career path that best matches their qualifications and experience. Clinical duties will include consultation, comprehensive functional assessment, diagnosis, treatment planning, and longitudinal follow-up for individuals with complex rehabilitation needs. Working within an interdisciplinary rehabilitation environment, the successful candidate will provide medical expertise and collaborate with rehabilitation professionals to optimize function, mobility, participation, pain management, and community reintegration. Clinical activities will include consultation and follow-up for individuals with: •          Stroke and acquired brain injury; •          Traumatic brain injury; •          Chronic neurological conditions; •          Post-polio syndrome; •          Neuro-musculoskeletal conditions; •          Chronic pain, including complex regional pain syndrome (CRPS); •          Severe motor impairments and mobility limitations; •          Complex mobility, positioning, and specialized assistive technology needs. Clinical interventions may include: •          Spasticity assessment and management, including injection therapies; •          Musculoskeletal and neuromuscular rehabilitation management; •          Ultrasound-guided and other image-guided peripheral joint injections; •          Prescription of orthoses, assistive devices, and adaptive equipment; •          Return-to-work planning and vocational rehabilitation support; •          Integration of tele-rehabilitation and virtual care approaches, where appropriate. The successful candidate will contribute to quality improvement, program development, clinical pathway design, program evaluation, and initiatives aimed at improving access, continuity of care, patient outcomes, and interdisciplinary rehabilitation practice. The position will support the continued development and expansion of physiatry services within Santé Québec Centre-Ouest-de-l'Île-de-Montréal – Universitaire, including initiatives that promote value-based rehabilitation care and the measurement of outcomes that matter to patients. The incumbent will also be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents and involvement in academic and administrative committees).   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates must: ​​​​Hold an MD degree and have completed specialty training in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation;  ​Be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent) in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation;  ​Be eligible for licensure in Quebec and certification by the Collège des médecins du Québec; Have completed at least one additional year of specialty clinical training in neurological rehabilitation. Additional training or experience in clinical research is considered an asset;   ​Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families and health care professionals.​  Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of date of posting The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter & curriculum vitae; A statement indicating your academic plans if you are successful in obtaining this position. Indicate your planned career path as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator. Describe your clinical time allocation and detail your teaching plans and plans for service to the academic community; The names and contact information of three referees. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Day-ChancellorHall/Student-Ambassadors-Ambassadeurs-tudiantes----AMM---EDI622_JR0000080690": {
      "title": "Student Ambassadors/Ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s  - AMM - EDI622",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Student Ambassadors – Admissions Office at the Faculty of Law at McGill 2026-2027 Ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s – Services des admissions à la Faculté de droit à McGill 2026-2027 The Faculty of Law’s Admissions Office is seeking to fill five part-time positions during the academic year for McGill law students. Candidates will be trained by and work with the Associate Dean (Admissions and Recruitment) and members of the Admissions Office team. Le Service des admissions de la Faculté de droit cherche à combler 5 postes d'emploi à temps partiel pendant l’année scolaire pour des étudiant(e)(s) en droit à McGill. Les candidat(e)(s) seront formé(e)s par et travailleront avec la Doyenne adjointe (admissions et recrutement) ainsi que les membres de l’équipe du Service des admissions. What is the Student Ambassador Program? This program connects admitted and prospective candidates with current law students. Ambassadors are uniquely placed to tell applicants about the advantages of studying law at McGill. In fact, many of our current ambassadors were themselves welcomed to the McGill Law community through this program. Student Ambassadors are also invited to attend alumni events and to participate in Homecoming or other semi-formal or formal functions. Qu'est-ce que le programme des étudiants ambassadeurs ? Ce programme met en relation les candidats admis et les futurs étudiants avec des étudiants en droit actuellement inscrits. Les ambassadeurs sont particulièrement bien placés pour présenter aux candidats les avantages d'étudier le droit à McGill. En effet, bon nombre de nos ambassadeurs actuels ont eux-mêmes été accueillis au sein de la communauté de la Faculté de droit de McGill grâce à ce programme. Les étudiants ambassadeurs sont également invités à assister à des événements organisés par les anciens élèves et à participer au Homecoming ou à d'autres réceptions semi-formelles ou formelles Position Summary Under the direct supervision of the Associate Dean (Admissions and Recruitment), the student ambassador will perform duties related to the recruitment of applicants and admitted students to the BCL/JD program. As representatives of the Faculty of Law, student ambassadors share their unique perspectives as current law students regarding the application and admissions process, the transition to life as a law student, and their knowledge of what McGill’s unique program has to offer. Résumé de la Position Sous la supervision directe de la Doyenne adjointe (admissions et recrutement), l'ambassadeur étudiant.e accomplira des tâches liées au recrutement de candidat.e.s et d'étudiant.e.s admis.es au programme BCL/JD. En tant que représentant.e.s de la Faculté de droit, les ambassadeurs étudiant.e.s partagent leurs perspectives uniques en tant qu'étudiant.e.s en droit actuel.le.s en ce qui concerne le processus de demande et d'admission, la transition vers le rôle d’etudiant.e en droit, ainsi que leurs connaissances de ce que le programme unique de McGill a à offrir. Description of Duties Greets students and answers general inquiries about the Faculty of Law (minors, majors, and joint degree programs, student life, living in Montreal, etc.) with a positive attitude. Hosts virtual and, when safety guidelines permit, in-person Faculty tours and class visits. In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Admissions Office, shares their student experience and answers questions from prospective and admitted students, their families, guidance counselors, and alumni during webinars, virtual events and, when safety guidelines permit, in-person events. In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Communications team, participates in social media campaigns and events (e.g. Instagram, Facebook and YouTube). In collaboration with the Faculty of Law’s Career Development Office, helps host on-campus events for prospective employers. Supports other recruitment and yield activities and events, as needed, such as answering the phone, responding to e-mails and assisting in various administrative tasks; helping with the setting up and taking down signs, equipment, etc. during on-campus events. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. Duration of Employment: Fall 2026 – Summer 2027 Weekly Hours: Weekly hours vary depending on event schedule and tasks assigned. When assigned, work will be 2 – 5 hours per week. Flexibility required. Qualifying Skills: Passive bilingualism General knowledge of the Faculty and student life Strong written and oral communication skills Strong interpersonal skills - must be willing to engage and initiate conversations with students and their families Involvement in the McGill community (clubs, services, athletics or other universityactivities) an asset Customer service and social media experience an asset All chosen Student Ambassadors will be required to complete a training given by the Admissions Office. Candidates should be bilingual, have facilitation and presentation experience and the ability to interact with various constituencies and groups. Prior experience with the Student Ambassador program or another program of its kind is a strong asset. Please upload your CV and short cover letter with the following information: Name and year of study (e.g., 1L, 2L, etc.) A brief bio/summary of your experiences prior to law Motivations for being a Student Ambassador Experience with work related to the duties described Plan for juggling other planned obligations/commitments for this next year alongside Student Ambassador work The 2nd document should not be more than 1 page and replaces the need for a cover letter. Description des tâches • Accueillir les étudiants et répondre aux demandes générales concernant la Faculté de droit (mineures, majeures et programmes de diplômes conjoints, vie étudiante, vie à Montréal, etc.) avec une attitude positive. • Animer des visites virtuelles de la Faculté et, lorsque les consignes de sécurité le permettent, des visites en personne ainsi que des visites de cours. • En collaboration avec le Bureau des admissions de la Faculté de droit, partager son expérience étudiante et répondre aux questions des étudiants potentiels et admis, de leurs familles, des conseillers en orientation et des diplômés lors de webinaires, d’événements virtuels et, lorsque les consignes de sécurité le permettent, d’événements en personne. • En collaboration avec l’équipe des communications de la Faculté de droit, participer à des campagnes et à des activités sur les médias sociaux (p. ex. Instagram, Facebook et YouTube). • En collaboration avec le Bureau du développement professionnel de la Faculté de droit, contribuer à l’organisation d’activités sur le campus destinées aux employeurs potentiels. • Soutenir, au besoin, d’autres activités et événements de recrutement et de conversion des candidats, notamment en répondant au téléphone, aux courriels et en effectuant diverses tâches administratives; contribuer à l’installation et au démontage des affiches, du matériel, etc. lors des événements sur le campus. • La liste des fonctions décrites ci-dessus est représentative et ne constitue pas une liste complète et détaillée des tâches qui peuvent être confiées à la personne titulaire du poste. Durée d’emploi Automne 2026 – été 2027 Heures hebdomadaires Les heures hebdomadaires varient en fonction de l’horaire des événements et des tâches assignées. Lorsqu’un travail est assigné, celui-ci représente de 2 à 5 heures par semaine. Flexibilité requise. Compétences recherchées • Bilinguisme passif • Connaissance générale de la Faculté et de la vie étudiante • Excellentes aptitudes en communication orale et écrite • Excellentes aptitudes interpersonnelles — la personne doit être à l’aise d’interagir avec les étudiants et leurs familles et de prendre l’initiative d’engager la conversation avec eux • Participation à la vie de la communauté mcgilloise (clubs, services, activités sportives ou autres activités universitaires), un atout • Expérience en service à la clientèle et dans les médias sociaux, un atout • Toutes les personnes retenues comme ambassadeurs étudiants devront suivre une formation offerte par le Bureau des admissions. Les candidats doivent être bilingues, posséder de l’expérience en animation et en présentation, ainsi que la capacité d’interagir avec différents publics et groupes. Une expérience antérieure dans le cadre du programme d’ambassadeurs étudiants ou d’un programme similaire constitue un atout important. Documents à soumettre Veuillez téléverser votre CV ainsi qu’une courte lettre de présentation contenant les renseignements suivants : • Nom et année d’études (p. ex. 1L, 2L, etc.) • Une courte biographie ou un résumé de vos expériences avant vos études en droit • Vos motivations à devenir ambassadeur étudiant • Votre expérience pertinente en lien avec les tâches décrites ci-dessus • Votre plan pour concilier vos autres obligations et engagements prévus au cours de la prochaine année avec votre travail d’ambassadeur étudiant Le deuxième document ne doit pas dépasser une page et remplace la nécessité de fournir une lettre de présentation distincte. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $17.17 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-08-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-22 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Tenure-Track-Position-in-Accounting--Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080360": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Accounting, Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-Track Position in Accounting Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Assistant Professor tenure-track position in the Accounting Area. The start date for the position is August 1, 2027 . The position is open to candidates with a Ph.D. in any area of specialization in Accounting , as well as to applicants demonstrating outstanding potential who are doctoral students and who plan to have completed the requirements for their Ph.D. by December 2027. Preference will be given to candidates whose research aligns with Faculty priority areas of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies; Innovation & Entrepreneurship; Sustainability; or Equity, Diversity & Inclusion. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses in financial accounting and management accounting and will demonstrate the ability to advance a cutting-edge, compelling and impactful research agenda. The ideal candidate will have evidence of effective teaching, an experience-appropriate record of research publications in high quality, peer-reviewed journals, and clear promise of conducting innovative and important research publishable in the top journals in accounting studies such as those listed on the Desautels 22 . Successful candidates are also expected to work actively with doctoral students, teach in our undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute to the administration of the Area and Faculty. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. The Accounting Area at Desautels brings together scholars specializing in the creation and dissemination of high-quality accounting research. Professors have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high level of independence in their research and teaching endeavors. McGill's emphasis is on research quality and academic and practitioner impact. The Faculty offers a Major and Concentration in Accounting at the undergraduate level, a joint honours program with Economics, and a Graduate Certificate in Professional Accounting (GCPA), an accredited CPA Quebec Professional Education Program preparing students to write the national Common Final Exam. The Area is also home to a PhD specialization in Accounting. Members of the Area are affiliated with the Business and Management Research Centre, a research incubator that helps develop and fund research initiatives through PhD seed grants, travel grants, and brownbag seminars. Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighbourhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for both individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world       Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels, teaching load, research funding and travel support are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday ( all in the same section as you submit your CV ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of current and prospective research When applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Sample research papers We are not requesting reference letters at this point; a reference check is a secondary step which may follow. All application materials should be submitted electronically by October 20, 2026 and only complete electronic submissions are accepted. The hiring process, including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to wuyang.zhao@mcgill.ca The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Prof. Wuyang Zhao Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Arts-Building/Assistant-Professor_JR0000080515": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-16\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty of Arts at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area: Democratic and community resilience Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to help support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years. Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment in the Department of Art History and Communication Studies at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 9, 2027 . Job Duties The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader will develop and pursue a research program aligned with the democratic and community resilience priority area. The successful candidate will also teach undergraduate and graduate courses, develop courses in their areas of specialization, supervise and mentor graduate students in the Department of Art History and Communication Studies, and contribute to the intellectual life of the University. Democratic and community resilience refers to the capacity of political institutions and communities to withstand and respond to pressures including rising authoritarianism and militarism, increasing economic precarity, declining trust in news media, environmental injustice, gendered, ableist, racist and other forms of prejudice and violence, and deteriorating conditions for diversity, equality, and freedom. Emerging communication and media technologies, including social media platforms, proliferating surveillance systems, cryptocurrencies, prediction markets, algorithmic decision-making and accelerating Artificial Intelligence are implicated in these pressures. A Canada Impact+ Emerging Leader will develop critical research and teaching programs that address the social, cultural, political, economic, infrastructural and environmental dimensions of emerging media, across regional, national and global contexts. Successful candidates will demonstrate their potential to contribute to intentional media policy, regulation and governance in the public interest, robust artistic and creative cultures, and/or vibrant community and political engagement with emerging media technologies. We seek candidates whose research centers the priorities, experiences and knowledges of historically and structurally disadvantaged and marginalized people, communities and regions, and who are committed to serving a diverse student body and an inclusive campus climate. Qualifications and Education Requirements Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 9, 2026, are eligible. Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible. Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. The category and period of eligible leaves should be clearly indicated in the required cover letter. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying .   Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system, using the link below. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday . Refer to the How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) job aid for instructions on how to apply. Required Documents The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB. Cover letter Curriculum vitae (including links to representative publications) Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (max. 1 page) Teaching statement and dossier (max. 1 page) Contact information for three references  Job Details Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience Posting Period Posting date: Wednesday September 16, 2026 Closing date: 30 days from posting date Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to Darin Barney, Professor and Chair, Department of Art History and Communication Studies ( darin.barney@mcgill.ca ). Commitment to Equity and Diversity Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . _____________________________________________________________ La Faculté des arts de l’Université McGill invite les candidatures pour un poste à temps plein de professeur adjoint menant à la permanence, destiné à soutenir le développement d’un programme de recherche transformationnel contribuant au rayonnement et au leadership international du Canada dans le domaine prioritaire stratégique suivant : Résilience démocratique et résilience des communautés La personne candidate doit disposer d’un programme de recherche indépendant démontrant un lien clair et stratégique avec ce domaine prioritaire. Le programme prévoit une contribution de 100 000 $ par année pendant six ans à l’Université McGill afin de soutenir les coûts salariaux associés à la nomination de la personne retenue ainsi que les dépenses directes de recherche. Une prolongation pouvant aller jusqu’à six années supplémentaires pourrait être accordée. La personne retenue devra entrer en fonction dans un poste à temps plein de professeur adjoint menant à la permanence au Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication , au plus tard le 9 juin 2027 . Responsabilités La personne recrutée dans le cadre des chaires de recherche Impact+ Canada devra élaborer et mener un programme de recherche en lien avec le domaine prioritaire de la r ésilience démocratique et communautaire. Elle devra également enseigner aux niveaux du premier cycle et des cycles supérieurs, concevoir et développer des cours correspondant à ses domaines de spécialisation, superviser, encadrer et accompagner des étudiants et étudiantes aux cycles supérieurs au sein du Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication, et contribuer activement à la vie intellectuelle et universitaire de McGill. La résilience démocratique et communautaire renvoie à la capacité des institutions politiques et des communautés à résister à différentes formes de pression et à y répondre, notamment la montée de l’autoritarisme et du militarisme, l’accroissement de la précarité économique, l’érosion de la confiance envers les médias d’information, les injustices environnementales, les préjugés et les violences fondés notamment sur le genre, le capacitisme et le racisme, ainsi que la dégradation des conditions favorisant la diversité, l’égalité et la liberté. Les technologies émergentes de communication et les nouveaux médias — notamment les plateformes de médias sociaux, la multiplication des systèmes de surveillance, les cryptomonnaies, les marchés prédictifs, la prise de décision algorithmique et le développement rapide de l’intelligence artificielle — jouent un rôle dans ces transformations et ces tensions. La personne retenue dans le cadre de Canada Impact+ devra développer des programmes de recherche et d’enseignement critiques portant sur les dimensions sociales, culturelles, politiques, économiques, infrastructurelles et environnementales des médias émergents, dans des contextes régionaux, nationaux et internationaux. Les candidatures seront particulièrement considérées en fonction du potentiel de la personne à contribuer à l’élaboration de politiques publiques, à la réglementation et à la gouvernance des médias dans l’intérêt public, au développement de cultures artistiques et créatives dynamiques, et/ou à la participation communautaire et politique autour des technologies médiatiques émergentes. Nous recherchons des personnes dont les recherches accordent une place centrale aux priorités, aux expériences et aux savoirs de personnes, de communautés et de régions historiquement et structurellement défavorisées ou marginalisées. Les candidates et candidats doivent également être engagés envers un milieu universitaire inclusif et envers l’accompagnement d’un corps étudiant diversifié. Qualifications et exigences Pour être admissibles, les candidates et candidats doivent avoir été établis à l’étranger, y travailler et y résider le 9 juin 2026. Les candidates et candidats doivent se situer dans les cinq années suivant le début de leur première nomination liée à la recherche, après déduction de la durée de toute interruption admissible de carrière en recherche. Les personnes récemment diplômées ainsi que les stagiaires postdoctoraux peuvent également être admissibles. Une nomination liée à la recherche est une nomination dans le cadre de laquelle la personne dispose de l’autonomie nécessaire pour mener des recherches de façon indépendante. Les congés admissibles — notamment les congés de maternité, parentaux, médicaux et de deuil — sont comptabilisés à deux fois leur durée réelle. La nature et la durée de tout congé admissible doivent être clairement indiquées dans la lettre de présentation. Les congés professionnels, notamment les congés de formation, sabbatiques ou administratifs, ne sont pas pris en compte. L’Université McGill exige un doctorat (Ph. D.) pour une nomination à un poste menant à la permanence. Toutefois, le doctorat ne constitue pas une exigence du programme des chaires de recherche Impact+ Canada Canada lui-même. Les candidates et candidats sont invités à consulter les critères d’ admissibilité et de sélection du programme Impact+ avant de présenter leur candidature. Les Canadiennes et Canadiens vivant à l’étranger qui souhaitent s’établir au Canada sont encouragés à présenter leur candidature. Processus de candidature Les candidatures doivent être soumises par l’intermédiaire de Workday, le système de gestion des ressources humaines de l’Université McGill, en utilisant le lien fourni ci-dessous. Lors de la création d’un compte Workday, veuillez utiliser une adresse courriel personnelle . Pour connaître les étapes à suivre, veuillez consulter le guide How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) . Documents exigés Le dossier de candidature doit inclure les documents suivants, regroupés en un seul fichier PDF d’une taille maximale de 5 Mo : une lettre de présentation; un curriculum vitæ, comprenant des liens vers des publications représentatives; un énoncé de recherche présentant les travaux actuels et futurs, leur adéquation avec le domaine prioritaire stratégique ainsi que leur potentiel en matière de traduction, de mobilisation et d’application des résultats de recherche ( 2 pages maximum ); une description des besoins en infrastructures de recherche , notamment en matière d’espace de laboratoire et d’équipement ( 1 page maximum ); un énoncé et dossier d’enseignement ( 1 page maximum ); les coordonnées de trois personnes pouvant fournir des références . Conditions du poste Type de poste : Poste menant à la permanence Rang : Professeur adjoint Salaire : Selon les qualifications et l’expérience. Période d’affichage Date d’affichage : Mercredi 16 septembre 2026 Date limite de candidature : 30 jours après la date d’affichage Les demandes d’information de la part de candidates et candidats potentiels sont les bienvenues et seront traitées en toute confidentialité. Les personnes intéressées peuvent communiquer avec Darin Barney, Professeur et directeur, Département d’histoire de l’art et d’études en communication, Université McGill ( darin.barney@mcgill.ca ). Engagement envers l’équité, la diversité et l’inclusion Pour travailler à l’Université McGill, vous devez être autorisé à travailler au Canada et être disposé à travailler au Québec, sur le campus où le poste est situé. L’Université McGill offre un soutien en matière d’immigration et de relocalisation aux personnes retenues. L’Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise. La majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’y déroulent en anglais; les personnes candidates doivent donc être en mesure de communiquer efficacement en anglais, tant à l’oral qu’à l’écrit. L’Université McGill est résolument engagée en faveur de l’équité et de la diversité au sein de sa communauté et accorde une grande importance à la rigueur et à l’excellence universitaires. Nous encourageons notamment les candidatures de personnes racisées et de minorités visibles, de femmes, de personnes autochtones, de personnes en situation de handicap, de membres de minorités ethniques et de personnes appartenant à des minorités en matière d’orientation sexuelle ou d’identité de genre. Toutes les personnes qualifiées possédant les compétences et les connaissances nécessaires pour contribuer de manière constructive à des communautés diversifiées sont également invitées à postuler. McGill valorise et encourage les recherches qui s’appuient sur une diversité de traditions intellectuelles, de méthodologies et de modes de diffusion et de mobilisation des connaissances. Les candidates et candidats sont invités à mettre en évidence les retombées de leurs travaux, tant à l’intérieur de leur discipline et entre différentes disciplines qu’auprès d’autres secteurs, notamment les gouvernements, les communautés et l’industrie. McGill reconnaît également l’incidence que peuvent avoir certains congés — notamment pour des raisons familiales ou de santé — sur la progression d’une carrière universitaire. Les candidates et candidats sont encouragés à signaler tout congé ayant pu entraîner une diminution de leur productivité ou avoir une incidence sur leur parcours professionnel. Ces renseignements seront pris en compte afin de favoriser une évaluation équitable du dossier. L’Université McGill applique un programme d’équité en matière d’emploi et encourage les membres des groupes désignés à s’auto-identifier. Elle s’efforce également de garantir le traitement équitable et la pleine inclusion des personnes en situation de handicap, notamment par l’application transversale des principes de conception universelle à l’ensemble de la vie universitaire ainsi que par ses politiques et procédures d’accommodement. Les personnes en situation de handicap qui prévoient avoir besoin de mesures d’adaptation à quelque étape que ce soit du processus de candidature peuvent communiquer en toute confidentialité avec Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MUHC-GLEN/Assistant-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Medicine--Division-of-Physical-Medicine-and-Rehabilitation--Palliative-Care--C2-260911-_JR0000080615": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Clinical), Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, Palliative Care (C2-260911)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description The McGill University Department of Medicine in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time position as a member of the Contract Academic Staff as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator at the rank of Assistant Professor (Clinical) based at the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC). This is a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. This position is conditional on the Institution successfully obtaining a PEM (i.e. physician permit) for this position. The Department of Medicine is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals. The McGill University Health Centre is one of McGill’s major teaching hospitals affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec. The Division of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation is highly committed to the pursuit of excellence in clinical care, research, and medical education. As a relatively small division, candidates will be expected to demonstrate an appreciation for the breadth of physiatry and have a vision for how to grow it while demonstrating an ability to work as part of a team.   Job Duties Please see the Department of Medicine (DOM) website for McGill DOM Career path definitions . Our career path definitions specify typical ranges of time allotted to clinical work, teaching (clinical and didactic), research, and administration. As a Clinician-Teacher, the successful candidate’s major non-clinical focus will be on teaching the next generation of physicians. Teaching activity will include both didactic and clinical teaching. As a Clinician-Educator, the successful candidate will focus on education research of sufficient caliber to publish in peer-reviewed journals or education administration with a focus on leading/developing world-class training programs. This position is focused on Palliative Care, an evolving area of practice within Physiatry. Clinical responsibilities include providing inpatient and ambulatory palliative care consultations at the adult sites of the McGill University Health Centre (MUHC), including the Royal Victoria Hospital and the Montreal General Hospital. Participation in on-call coverage within this area of practice is expected. The successful candidate will also contribute to the weekly Cancer Pain Clinic. Additional opportunities may be available to participate in consultations through the Alan Edwards Pain Management Unit and in general physiatry services at the Montreal General Hospital. The incumbent will be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees). McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements Candidates must: Hold an MD degree; Have completed specialty training in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation; Be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent) in Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation; Be eligible for licensure in Quebec and certification by the Collège des médecins du Québec ; Have at least one additional year of clinical training in Palliative Care or Pain Management ; Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families and health care professionals. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Medicine, Division of Physical Medicine & Rehabilitation Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of date of posting The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter & curriculum vitae; A statement indicating your academic plans if you are successful in obtaining this position. Indicate your planned career path as a Clinician-Teacher or Clinician-Educator .   Describe your clinical time allocation and detail your teaching plans and plans for service to the academic community; The names and contact information of three referees Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer-EDEC-277-760-OFNIE-F26_JR0000080682": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEC 277 760 OFNIE F26",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEC 277 760 Mohawk Language 1 Course Description: Students will learn the Mohawk phonological system (including glottal stop, length mark, up and down stress). Syntactically and morphologically, they will focus on the pronoun system (tense included). Word generation conventions will be analyzed and Mohawk labels developed to describe how the language functions. Posting Dates : September 17 - 30, 2026 Application Deadline: September 30, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 3 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 1 Term: Fall 2026 Course Dates : November 23 - December 18, 2026 Contract dates : November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027 Schedule: Monday, Tuesday, & Thursday: 6:00 p.m. - 9:15 p.m. Location: Kahnawà:ke Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being giving in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum and/or instructional approaches grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld.  The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered.  Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are November 9, 2026 - January 1, 2027. Course dates are November 23 - December 18, 2026. This course will be offered in person in Kahnawà:ke following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.  Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l’éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuit (OFNIE) Course Title: Langue mohawk 1 Subject Code: EDEC 277 760 Location: Kahnawà꞉ke Schedule: Lundi, mardi et jeudi, de 18 h à 21 h 15 Deadline to Apply: 2026-09-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Research-Assistant---SGI--Sustainable-Africa-_JR0000080726-1": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - SGI (Sustainable Africa)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: This project is a Research-to-Action that seeks to explore linkages between nutrition and agri-food businesses in local and global agri-food/public health supply networks, with a focus on linkages and gaps in target geographies: selected districts/regions in Ghana, with linkages to other African countries, starting with Ivory Coast, Senegal, and with linkages to Canada.  The project is situated within the broader goals of the Sustainable Growth Initiative (SGI) Office of Sustainable Africa and the Nkabom Collaborative, a multi-stakeholder partnership with the Mastercard Foundation that brings together McGill University, six Ghanaian educational institutions, and an industry partner to transform Ghana’s nutrition and agri-food ecosystem by creating opportunities for youth-led impact through experiential learning, innovation, and entrepreneurship. The project will generate evidence-based insights and actionable recommendations to inform SGI programming, institutional partnerships, policy engagement, and future project development focused on sustainable nutrition and agri-food systems.  Project Purpose  The purpose of this research is to understand how entrepreneurship and public health supply networks interact to support or constrain the sustainability of nutrition and agri-food organizations and the project will:  1. Map relevant public health and entrepreneurship initiatives in the target countries.   2. Identify connections and gaps between these initiatives and nutrition and agri-food businesses.   3. Examine opportunities for stronger collaboration among academia, industry, government, and development organizations.   4. Develop recommendations to support SGI programming, partnerships, policy engagement, and future implementation initiatives.  Role Description   The Research Assistant will support the project by:  1. Conducting literature reviews on entrepreneurship, nutrition and agri-food businesses, public health supply networks, and sustainable enterprise development.   2. Mapping relevant initiatives, businesses, institutions, and ecosystem stakeholders.   3. Supporting stakeholder analysis and identifying existing partnerships and gaps.  4. Assisting with research and data collection.   5. Analyzing findings to identify barriers, opportunities, and potential solutions.   6. Developing a business model framework for strengthening the sustainability and growth of nutrition and agri-food organizations.   7. Preparing reports, presentations, briefing notes, and other knowledge translation materials.   8. Participating in fortnightly project meetings and supporting project coordination.    Qualifications: The successful candidate should:  1. Be enrolled as a student at McGill University during the period of employment (Undergraduate, Master's, or PhD).   2. Be available to work from September 2026 through December 2027.   3. Demonstrate an interest in entrepreneurship, agriculture, nutrition, public health, sustainable development, supply chains, or related fields.   4. Demonstrate strong research, communication, analytical, and organizational skills.   5. Be able to conduct literature reviews and stakeholder mapping.   6. Have prior coursework or experience in entrepreneurship, policy, innovation, agriculture, nutrition, food systems, or related fields (preferred).   7. Be able to work independently while contributing effectively to an interdisciplinary team.  Supervision & Support  This Research Assistant will work under the supervision of Prof. Julie Ricard and John Gradek and will receive:  1. Academic oversight and mentorship.   2. Structured guidance through fortnightly progress meetings.   3. Access to SGI networks and relevant stakeholders.   4. Support in translating research findings into practical recommendations and implementation opportunities.  Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Location: Bronfman Building Supervisor: Associate Professor (Professional) Position Start Date: 2026-09-28 Position End Date: 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Tenure-Track-Position-in-Strategy---Organization--Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080691": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Strategy & Organization, Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-Track Position in Strategy & Organization Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Assistant Professor tenure-track position in the Strategy & Organization (S&O) Area. The start date for the position is August 1, 2027 . The position is open to candidates with a Ph.D. in any area of specialization relevant to strategy and organization, as well as to applicants demonstrating outstanding potential who are doctoral students and who plan to have completed the requirements for their Ph.D. by December 2027. Preference will be given to candidates whose research aligns with Faculty priority areas of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies; Innovation & Entrepreneurship; Sustainability; or Equity, Diversity & Inclusion. How organizations define, pursue, govern, and sustain forms of value that cannot be reduced to short-term economic performance may be the most pressing challenges of our time. Social impact is no longer a peripheral concern, but increasingly central to how the next generation of managers will be educated. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses focusing on the social impact of strategic management and organizations ; and will demonstrate the ability to advance a cutting-edge, compelling and impactful research agenda. Social impact in strategic management is core to the intellectual identity of the Strategy and Organization area, where colleagues engage with programs focused on how organizations formulate direction, coordinate complex activity, respond to institutional pressures, and create value across multiple stakeholder groups. The ideal candidate will have evidence of effective teaching, an experience-appropriate record of research publications in high quality, peer-reviewed journals, and clear promise of conducting innovative and important research publishable in the top journals in strategy and organization studies such as those listed on the Desautels 22 . Successful candidates are also expected to work actively with doctoral students, to teach in our undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute to the administration of the Area and Faculty. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. The Strategy and Organization Area at Desautels brings together scholars specializing in strategic management, organizational theory, and the study of globalization, industry emergence, and organizational change. Professors have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high level of independence in their research and teaching endeavors. McGill's emphasis is on research that combines theoretical and empirical rigour with social relevance, often addressing \"big questions\" through longitudinal, process-focused study. The Faculty offers a Major in Strategic Management at the undergraduate level, along with concentrations in Social Business & Enterprise and Global Strategy, as well as an MBA concentration in Global Strategy and Leadership and a PhD specialization in Strategy and Organization. Members of the Area are affiliated with the Centre for Strategy Studies in Organizations (CSSO), which promotes research on the formation of strategic direction, innovation in organizational forms and practices, and the relationship between organizations and society. Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighbourhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for both individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world       Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels, teaching load, research funding and travel support are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday ( all in the same section as you submit your CV ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of current and prospective research When applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Sample research papers We are not requesting reference letters at this point; reference check is a secondary step which may follow.      All application materials should be submitted electronically by October 20, 2026 and only complete electronic submissions are accepted. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the positions are filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to saku.mantere@mcgill.ca The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Prof. Saku Mantere Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Grader---MGSC-603_JR0000080719": {
      "title": "Grader - MGSC 603",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). A grader provides support with marking exams and assignments for a particular course.  Background knowledge of the subject matter is an asset.   Hourly Salary: $16.60 Position Start Date: 2026-09-13 Position End Date: 2027-01-16 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Post-Doctoral-Researcher_JR0000080714": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-17\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences Position summary: The Department of Atmospheric and Oceanic Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a postdoctoral scholar in atmospheric moist convection. The objective is to develop reduced but realistic models of cumulus life cycles that may be applied toward cumulus parameterization and/or machine-learning algorithms for predicting short-term convective hazards. The model development will be aided by synthesizing observational data and high-resolution numerical simulations to gain new conceptual insights on cloud life cycles. Potential observational sources include data from field campaigns, long-term observational deployments, and operational satellite and radar. The numerical experiments will include large-eddy simulations over large domains, where the clouds and their interactions with mesoscale circulations are simultaneously resolved. The candidate will join a dynamic research group focused on convective-scale processes and their representation in atmospheric prediction models. The initial appointment is for a term of one year but may be extended upon satisfactory performance and available funding. Job description: Using large-eddy simulations and observations for guidance, build a quantitative conceptual understanding of the critical factors controlling cumulus life cycles Develop and critically evaluate new physical model(s) for cumulus life cycles based on the above findings Publish results in high-impact peer-reviewed journals Qualifying Skills: Ph.D. in meteorology, atmospheric science, or closely related field Experience in the study of clouds and cumulus convection Strong conceptual understanding of cloud microphysical and turbulent processes Expertise in the use of modern scientific visualization software (Python, Matlab, etc.) Demonstration of scientific productivity, as evidenced by journal articles and other published work Demonstration of scientific communication, primarily through conference and workshop presentations Application materials Up-to-date curriculum vitae Contact information for three professional references Postdocs at McGill must remain within 5 years of being awarded their Ph.D. throughout their appointment. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Annual Salary: $62,000.00 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Burnside Hall Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-11-01 Position End Date: 2027-10-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-17 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Music-527-Sherbrooke/Liaison-Librarian_JR0000080903": {
      "title": "Liaison Librarian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Assistant Librarian or Associate Librarian (tenure track) Duration: three years, with possibility of renewal Salary: salary commensurate with experience   The Marvin Duchow Music Library (MDML) seeks a service-oriented and highly collaborative professional liaison librarian to lead a range of library and information services and collections to support teaching, learning, research, and outreach activities, with particular attention to the needs of graduate students of the Schulich School of Music.   This position is responsible for cultivating strong partnerships with academic departments, delivering specialized services in information literacy and resource discovery, and developing collections that reflect curricular and research needs. The librarian contributes to a collaborative environment and ensures that library services are responsive, inclusive, and aligned with institutional goals.   Constituted in 1965, the Marvin Duchow Music Library is one of Canada’s leading academic music libraries. Its collections of over 200,000 music scores, recordings, books, and journals are complemented by extensive online resources in various formats.   As an embedded library within the McGill Libraries system, it provides resources and services to support the performance, research, and teaching programs of the Schulich School of Music . The Library also serves the broader McGill University community, as well as academic libraries across Quebec and beyond.     THE McGILL LIBRARIES   Located in Montréal, one of the world’s great multicultural and multilingual cities, McGill University is internationally recognized for its excellence as a leading institution of higher education and research. For nearly 200 years, through the work of dedicated people, McGill has been breaking ground in diverse fields and contributing solutions to some of the world’s most significant issues. McGill is also the most internationally diverse research-intensive university in Canada, with over 32% of its students coming to McGill from more than 150 countries. The University systematically appears on the annual list of the best employers in Montreal.   The McGill University Libraries contribute to society by advancing teaching, learning, and research in a supportive and welcoming environment. We anticipate and respond to the needs of users by providing outstanding collections, excellent services, and spaces that inspire. We work in collaboration with global scholarly and library communities to enhance and facilitate access to knowledge.   The McGill Libraries has the largest collection in Quebec and is one of Canada’s largest academic libraries with over six million monograph titles and 150,000 e-journals and databases. The Libraries are a member of the Canadian Association of Research Libraries (CARL), the Association of Research Libraries (ARL), the Partenariat des bibliothèques universitaires du Québec (PBUQ), the Center for Research Libraries (CRL), and the HathiTrust Digital Library.     INFORMATION FOR PROSPECTIVE STAFF   Information about the University and the McGill Libraries can be found on the University’s web site. The McGill Libraries have developed Strategic Priorities for 2025-2030 to guide its actions. As a tenure stream appointee, the successful candidate is expected to satisfy the requirements in the Regulations Relating to the Employment of Librarian Staff. DUTY STATEMENT   Primary Purpose of Position   The assist the provision of a range of library and information services and collections to support teaching, learning, research, and outreach activities   Duties and responsibilities include, but are not limited to:   Participate actively in the co-leading and development of the Marvin Duchow Music Library’s Information Literacy Program, ensuring currency, cyclical reviews, and alignment with latest trends in teaching and learning in higher education. Conduct information literacy/skills classes and training programs for users and library staff, with a specific focus on doctoral program candidates and instructors. Provide expert guidance, consultation, and research support to on-site and remote users in discovering, accessing, and effectively using library and information resources for teaching, learning, and research in music. Develop and maintain communication links and appropriately liaise with designated academic departments and university centres to ensure library and information needs are understood and met. May coordinate services and supervise the work of other library staff in designated areas, fostering collaboration, ensuring service quality, and working effectively as a member of a team. Develop, produce, and maintain library documentation, including web content, to support pedagogy, user engagement, and resource discovery. May assist in the development of the MDML collections in specific disciplinary areas to ensure teaching, learning, and research needs are met. Promote the Libraries’ resources and services and assist in outreach activities as appropriate. Work in partnership with other Libraries and University staff and sections, as well as external groups, on specific projects. Serve on various library, faculty, and university committees and represent the library on external committees, as required. Maintain current awareness of emerging technologies and developments in the library, education, and information environments and develop possible responses. Engage in scholarly activities and professional service and develop a dossier for promotion and tenure, consistent with University Regulations.  The duties listed above are representative and not exhaustive; responsibilities may change or evolve in response to institutional and library needs.   Reporting relationship   This position reports to the Head Librarian, Marvin Duchow Music Library. SELECTION CRITERIA   Required: A Master’s Degree in Library or Information Studies (MLIS, MIS, or MI) and a degree in music. Experience serving library users in an academic library or similar setting. Knowledge of and experience with the use of relevant music and other information resources, including electronic and networked resources, and information and communications technology. Knowledge of and experience with music research, and with music research methods and tools. Demonstrated ability to conduct information skills classes for small and large groups at various levels of study, preferably including experience with graduate cohorts Excellent organizational, documentation, and knowledge management skills. Proven ability to work in collaborative settings, including participation in situation analysis, problem solving, and group decision making. Capacity to supervise the work of other staff and coordinate workflows. Relationship building skills and experience creating and fostering a positive working environment. High level of professionalism, commitment to the organization and its mission, and the ability to work flexibly in a changing environment. Ability to understand and respond to priorities and trends in library and university environments. Demonstrated ability to uphold the values of respect, collaboration, diversity, and employment equity. Fluency in oral and written English is required. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at level 4 on a scale of 1-4.     Desired: A graduate degree in music, including coursework in musicology is highly desired. Professional experience in an academic music library or similar setting is highly desirable. Oral and written abilities in French are highly desired. Working knowledge of German or Italian is an asset.     The McGill Libraries are committed to advancing open, equitable, and sustainable research practices and welcomes candidates who will contribute to these priorities.   APPLICATION PROCEDURE   Applications should address the above selection criteria, be accompanied by: a letter of interest, a curriculum vitae, the names and addresses of three referees.     *** Applicants are encouraged to indicate any accommodation needs related to job duties. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Faculty-Lecturer--Department-of-Bioengineering_JR0000080506": {
      "title": "Faculty Lecturer- Department of Bioengineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Faculty Lecturer, Department of Bioengineering The Department of Bioengineering at McGill University invites applications for a full-time Faculty Lecturer position in Bioengineering. The appointment will begin with an initial two-year term and may subsequently lead to an indefinite appointment, subject to performance and University policies. The candidate will have the expertise to teach both undergraduate and graduate courses in bioengineering. McGill University is among the top research-intensive academic institutions in Canada, attracting over one-half billion dollars in competitive research funding each year. It consistently ranks in the top universities worldwide. The successful candidate will play leading roles in maintaining McGill’s international reputation of excellence in teaching, and service. Established in 2012, the Department of Bioengineering is the newest department to join McGill University’s renowned Faculty of Engineering. ​The Department focuses on three major directions: biological materials and mechanics; biomolecular and cellular engineering; and biomedical diagnostics and high throughput screening. The Department is committed to excellence in teaching in its undergraduate and graduate programs including B.Eng. in Bioengineering, M.Sc. and Ph.D. in Biological and Biomedical Engineering, and M.Eng. (non-thesis) in Biomanufacturing and values the service contributions of its Faculty members to the University, the profession, and society at large.  The successful candidate will play a leading role in teaching Bioengineering undergraduate and graduate courses. Please refer to https://www.mcgill.ca/bioengineering/studies for a complete listing of the courses offered. The expected start date is: December 1, 2026. Duties and Responsibilities: Duties performed by the Faculty Lecturer-include (but are not limited to) the following: Contribute to the development and delivery of the undergraduate and graduate courses and programs. Teach credit courses at both the undergraduate and graduate level – a minimum of 15 credits per academic year, and identify appropriate assessment tools for each course. Ensure that course content and delivery maintain high academic standards Implement equity, diversity, and inclusion (EDI) considerations in teaching and learning activities. Review course evaluations and identify and propose areas of improvement Ensure that university and department policies, guidelines, and procedures are met. Serve on the departmental Program Committee and on other committees as needed. Participate in communication with appropriate professional associations and business organizations to ensure that departmental programs are in alignment with industry needs. Support and be involved in other activities or projects as required, e.g., grant applications, supervision of teaching labs. Contribute to the development of on-line versions for selected courses related to the M.Eng. biomanufacturing program and M.Sc. concentration in Synthetic Biology Qualifications and Education Requirements: Candidates must have a PhD in Chemical, Biochemical or Biological Engineering. Candidates with direct experience in bioprocessing and manufacturing of biologics including advanced process analytical technologies and process digitalization will be preferred. Candidates with training and experience in bioinformatics and statistical analysis will be preferred. Evidence of experience in outstanding teaching activities at university level A minimum of five years of professional experience in industry, public sector, or academia. Membership or eligibility for the membership in a Canadian professional engineering association. Demonstrated skills in educational needs assessment and all aspects of program development and delivery. Demonstrated strong organizational skills. Interest and demonstrated ability to keep abreast of advances in the field of Biomanufacturing and Life Sciences and Artificial Intelligence. Dedication to an environment that supports equity, diversity, and inclusion to address in their application their ability to teach and mentor in this context. Strong record of scientific contributions. Registration in a Canadian professional engineering association is required within the first 5 years following hiring. Job Type: Contract Academic Staff Rank: Faculty Lecturer Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of the publication of this advertisement. Required Documents: The following supporting documents are required and should be uploaded through Workday: A curriculum vitae A teaching statement that includes a description of your teaching philosophy and interests, Samples of teaching materials, e.g., a course outline from a course developed and taught, and where available, evidence of teaching effectiveness, e.g., student course evaluations A statement on equity, diversity and inclusion The names and email addresses of three references For inquiries please contact chair.bioeng@mcgill.ca Length of Appointment: 2 years (with the possibility of extension) Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Tenure-Track-Position-in-Finance--Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080986": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Position in Finance, Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-Track Position in Finance Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one tenure-track position at the level of Assistant Professor in the Finance Area. The start date for the position is August 1, 2027 . The position is open to candidates with a Ph.D in Finance, Economics, or a related field as well as to applicants demonstrating outstanding potential who are doctoral students in those fields and plan to have completed the requirements for their Ph.D. by December 2027. The position is open to candidates in any area of specialization within finance including candidates whose research interests relate to the Desautels faculty priority areas of Innovation, Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies, Sustainability, or Equity, Diversity & Inclusion. Successful candidates will demonstrate the ability to advance a cutting-edge, compelling, and impactful research agenda. The ideal candidate will have evidence of effective teaching, an experience-appropriate record of research, and clear promise of conducting innovative and important research publishable in the top finance journals such as those listed on the Desautels 22 . Successful candidates are also expected to work actively with doctoral students, to teach in our undergraduate and graduate programs, and contribute to the administration of the Area and Faculty. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. Faculty members in the Finance Area conduct research with a variety of approaches in asset pricing, corporate finance, banking and financial institutions, macro finance, FinTech, and many other areas of finance. Professors have diverse disciplinary backgrounds and enjoy a high level of independence in their research and teaching endeavors. McGill’s emphasis is on research quality, innovation, and impact. Faculty members have an opportunity to contribute to the activities of several Research Institutes and Centres, including the Desmarais Global Finance Research Centre. Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighbourhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for both individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world       Desautels Faculty of Management   Compensation levels, teaching load, research funding, and travel support are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday ( all in the same section as you submit your CV ): Cover letter Curriculum vitae Statement of current and prospective research When applicable, evidence of teaching effectiveness Sample research papers   Additionally, three letters of reference should be sent via email to finance.mgmt@mcgill.ca . All application materials should be submitted electronically by Friday, November 13, 2026 and only complete electronic submissions are accepted. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to finance.mgmt@mcgill.ca The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: David Schumacher, Area Director (Finance) Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Dental-Assistant_JR0000080941": {
      "title": "Dental Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Dental Assistant Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, assists staff and students in the treatment of patients. Assists in the instruction of dental students. Prepares and sets up instruments and materials. Ensures compliance with infection control. Schedules appointments. Compile reports. Ensures maintenance and supply of equipment and materials.   Duties and Responsibilities: Laboratory tasks requiring to pour models / trim models for lab exercises. Number of models often exceeding 100. Demonstrate to students how to pour models / trim models. Work with extracted teeth. Take radiographs on extracted teeth. Oral surgery tasks requiring to work with animal parts – tongues / jaws. Help setting up practical exams. Help maintaining all equipment in simulation lab / plaster room. Clean the plaster room with the help of the students. Guide students with their practical requirements – appliances / dentures / custom trays etc. Take alginate impressions Pour impressions – knowledge of different stones. Type 1 / 2 /3 /4 / stone Trim models to specifications Mount models on articulator Fabricate surgical stent – surgical guide Knowledge of denture teeth – choose correct molds Fabricate custom trays – border molding / wax rims / teeth placement Fabricate ortho appliances – Essix retainers Placement of bands & bracketts for ortho Fabricate athletic mouth guards Fabricate bleaching trays (whitening) Fabricate vacu shells for temporization Use putty for temporization Wax up teeth The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other qualifying skills: Dental Assistant Certification is a requirement Knowledge of dental laboratory procedures is considered an asset High degree of manual dexterity. Strong organizational and time management skills, with the ability to prioritize tasks effectively English and French spoken and written. Ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a team environment Proven ability to perform under pressure in a fast-paced, multi-tasking setting Ability to spend long hours standing, stretching, bending and light lifting.   As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Dental Assistance 3 Years Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level D) $26.18 - $32.46 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Administration Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-07 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Penfield-740/Student-Research-Assistant_JR0000079403": {
      "title": "Student Research Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Centre for Genomics and Policy, Department of Human Genetics Supervisor: Prof. Ma’n H. Zawati Position Summary The Human Genetics research team is seeking a highly motivated Student Research Assistant to support research activities related to the legal, ethical, social, and policy dimensions of genomic research, precision medicine, and open science initiatives. The successful candidate will work closely with Professor Ma’n H. Zawati and collaborate with researchers at the Centre of Genomics and Policy. This position offers an excellent opportunity for students interested in health law, health policy, bioethics, genomics, privacy regulation, and interdisciplinary research. Key Responsibilities The Student Research Assistant will: Conduct literature reviews on legal, social, ethical, and policy issues related to genomic research, precision medicine, and health data sharing. Analyze and compare provincial, national, and international legal and policy frameworks. Assist in the development and drafting of policy documents and recommendations for a variety of stakeholders, including researchers, healthcare institutions, ethics boards, professionals, and the public. Support the research, drafting, copy-editing, citation verification, and source-checking of manuscripts submitted to peer-reviewed journals in law, bioethics, and science. Participate in project meetings and contribute to team discussions. Support teaching-related activities, as appropriate. Collaborate with members of the Centre of Genomics and Policy and other research partners. Perform additional research-related duties as assigned by the supervisor. Qualifications Currently pursuing a Law degree with strong academic standing; or Currently pursuing an undergraduate or graduate degree in sociology, anthropology, political science, psychology, public health, counselling psychology, ethics, bioethics, or a related discipline. Demonstrated interest in health law and policy, health research regulation, regulation of health professionals, emerging technologies, and/or privacy law. Strong research, analytical, and written communication skills. Excellent attention to detail and organizational skills. Ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a collaborative team. Fluency in English and working knowledge of French. Assets Familiarity with comparative law methodologies. Experience with copy-editing, reference management, and legal source-checking. Background or coursework in biology, genomics, bioinformatics, health data science, or bioethics. Work Schedule Minimum commitment of 14 hours per week during the academic year. Flexible work schedule based on student availability and project requirements. Opportunity for increased hours, including full-time work, during the summer period. This is an in-person position based at McGill University. Language requirement: Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Hours per Week: 14 (Part time) Location: Penfield 740 Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-07 Position End Date: 2027-08-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Tenure-Track-Faculty-Position-in-Computer-Science--Quantum-Computing_JR0000080250": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Faculty Position in Computer Science: Quantum Computing",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The School of Computer Science at McGill University (Montreal, Canada) invites applications for a tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor. We are seeking candidates with a PhD (or close to finishing) in Computer Science and expertise at the intersection of quantum computing and computer science. Topics of interest include, but are not limited to foundational and applied quantum algorithms, quantum cryptography, quantum information theory, quantum programming languages, quantum compilation, and classical-quantum computer architectures. Position Description: The School of Computer Science at McGill University (Montreal, Canada) invites applications for a tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor. The expected start date is August 1, 2027. Job Duties: The successful candidate will be expected to conduct research and secure competitive external funding, teach a diverse body of undergraduate and graduate students, and provide service to the University, the profession, and society at large. Qualifications and Education Requirements: PhD (or close to finishing) in Computer Science and expertise at the intersection of quantum computing and computer science. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Science / School of Computer Science Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period: The deadline to submit your application is until November 14, 2026 . The selection process will begin November 15, 2026, and continue until the positions are filled. Required Documents: A complete application package must include: A cover letter A Curriculum Vitae including a list of publications A selection of 3-5 publications that the candidate considers as major and representative of their research activity A research statement A teaching statement The names and email addresses of three references For external applicants, a guide on the application process is available . For more information, contact brigitte.pientka@mcgill.ca   The School of Computer Science at McGill University consistently ranks among the top Computer Science departments in Canada. It offers a collegial environment with opportunities for interactions with world-class researchers in areas ranging from software engineering, programming languages and distributed systems to artificial intelligence and robotics. McGill University is among the top research-intensive academic institutions in Canada and has consistently been ranked in the top universities worldwide. Montreal is a historic and cosmopolitan city, home to four universities, and is considered one of the top three cities for students in North America. COMMITMENT TO EQUITY AND DIVERSITY McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through accommodation policies and procedures. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, tynan.jarrett@mcgill.ca     All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
