    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Teaching-Assistant---Introduction-to-Pharmacology-2---PHAR-201---Winter-2027_JR0000081094-1": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - Introduction to Pharmacology 2 - PHAR 201 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pharmacology & Therapeutics Course Title: Introduction to Pharmacology 2 Course Code: PHAR 201 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 45 Position Summary: Attendance at classes. Running tutorials. Setting up and invigilating exams. Marking of midterm and final exams. Review of exams with students. Help with posting files to myCourses website for the course.       .      Qualifications: Graduate student who has successfully passed PHAR 562 and PHAR 563 or the equivalent.  Must have sufficient command of English to be able to help international students.  Very good communication skills and attention to detail.  Must be comfortable in marking the midterm and final short answer questions.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Raymond-Building/Assistant-Professor-in-Regulated-Plant-Pests----Canada-Impact--Emerging-Leaders_JR0000081085": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Regulated Plant Pests -  Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-25\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders (McGill University) Tenure-Track Faculty Position Open to rank of Assistant Professor in Regulated Plant Pests   Program Description   The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world.   The Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure-track Assistant Professor position to advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the following strategic priority area:   Environment, climate resilience, and the Arctic   Emerging Leaders must have an independent program of research that demonstrates strategic alignment with this priority area. The award provides $100,000 per year for six years to McGill University to support the salary costs associated with the successful Emerging Leader appointment as well as direct research expenses, with the possibility of extension for a further six years.   Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than June 28, 2027 , in the Department of Plant Science.   Job Duties   The successful candidate specializes in the study of insects, pathogens, nematodes, or invasive weeds that are legally restricted by governments to protect agriculture and ecosystems. The successful candidate will develop an internationally recognized, externally funded research program focused on the biology, diagnostics, epidemiology, management or responses to environmental and climate change of regulated pests, invasive species, or quarantine organisms that affect agricultural crops in Canada.   The successful candidate is expected to teach core courses [such as an introductory course related to plant pests (e.g., Insect Biology), Pesticides in Agriculture] in the Faculty’s undergraduate programs and a graduate course related to Integrated Pest Management. In addition to teaching, the candidate will be expected to conduct innovative research in regulated plant pests, making full use of the extensive research infrastructure at the Macdonald Campus of McGill University, more specifically the Plant Pest Containment (PPC) Laboratory, which is part of the Eastern Canadian Plant Phenotyping Platform (ECP3). The ideal candidate will bridge the gap between academic research and regulatory science, collaborating closely with government agencies, industry stakeholders, and international plant health organizations.   The candidate should also actively pursue collaborative research with other departments and schools within the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences to foster interdisciplinary approaches. Participation in Department, Faculty, and University committees is expected, as are interactions with provincial and federal organizations and professional societies.   Qualifications and Education Requirement s   Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on June 28, 2026, are eligible.   Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible.   Ph.D. in Plant Health, Plant Biology, Integrated Pest Management or related field; track record and demonstrated excellence in working in high-containment facilities (Biosafety Level 2 or 3) handling quarantine or regulated pests; knowledge of Canadian plant protection legislation, the Plant Protection Act, and international phytosanitary standards (ISPMs).   McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The ability to teach and communicate in French and prior experience with the Canadian agricultural and agri-food industry are considered an asset.   Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently.   Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited.   A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself.   Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying.     Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences / Department of Plant Science Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period: Please submit your application by October 30, 2026. Required Documents: The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB.   Cover letter Curriculum vitae  Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (max. 1 page) Teaching statement (max. 1 page) and dossier Contact information for three references    Application Process   Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system.   Posting Period   Review of applications will start on November 1, 2026. Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to Prof. Martin Filion ( martin.filion@mcgill.ca ), chair of the search committee. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located.   Commitment to Equity and Diversity   Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.   At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry.   McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.   McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca .   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Invigilator_JR0000080575": {
      "title": "Invigilator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-24\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biology Exam Period: BIOL 111 Monday, October 19, 2026 7:30-9:00 pm (LEA 132; LEA219, LEA232, LEA26 (and SAA). BIOL 115 Tuesday, October 20, 2026 7:30-9:30 pm (ADAMS AUD, MCMED 504, STBIO/S1/4). BIOL 215 Tuesday, October 20, 2026 7:30-9:30 pm (ENGMC 204, 304). BIOL 200 Wednesday, October 21, 2026 7:00 - 8:30 pm (LEA219, LEA26, LEA232, ARTS W-120, ARTS W-215, MCMED 521, MCMED 522, MAASS 112, MAASS 10, ADAMS AUD, ENGMC 13, ENGMC 304). BIOL200 (Deferred midterm) Monday, October 26, 2026 6:30-8:00 pm (STBIO N2/2 and S1/3). BIOL306 Monday, October 26, 2026 6:00-9:00 (MCMED 1034, 522). BIOL302 Tuesday, October 27, 2026 7:30-9:00 pm (MCMED 504). BIOL219 Tuesday, November 10, 2026 6:30-9:00 pm (S1/3, S3/3). Please also take into account setup, takedown and training time, which will be added. Position Summary: Responsible for setting up the examination rooms for mid-term exams according to specific requirements. Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Alerts professor when students have a question or a concern, if arises. At the end of the examination, period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensure delivery of the exams. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Understanding and commitment to maintaining appropriate standards of confidentiality and security of materials. Adhering to McGill University policies and procedures. Ability to work independently; Dependability, reliability, punctuality. Education/Experience: Invigilating experience an asset. An Employee that holds more than one position at the University must inform their Supervisor in writing, on the application form or during the course of the Employee’s appointment, of any other assignments presently held at the University.   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (TSUM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-04 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
