    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistant---GPHL-300---W2027_JR0000082510-1": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - GPHL 300 - W2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Global and Public Health Course Title: Data Literacy Course Code: GPHL 300 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Position Summary: Meet periodically with course supervisors Clarify course content and assignments with students Attend regular Zoom lectures Assist with evaluation/grading Assist with online course management Help prepare and facilitate class discussions, media sharing, and group work Uphold academic integrity and communicate concerns with course supervisors Respond to or share email communications from students with course supervisors as appropriate Qualifications: PhD candidate or 2nd-year Master’s student Organized and efficient with good time management skills Attention to detail regarding all course aspects Approachable and helpful Familiar with course materials and assignments Background or familiarity with data literacy concepts, including statistical thinking, data visualization, and interpretation of datasets in relation to real-world health and population phenomena Comfortable assisting students in developing confidence with data-driven reasoning, graph interpretation, and foundational data science or epidemiological principles Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required to be on campus to perform their tasks in person. How to Apply To be eligible for a Teaching Assistant position you must be registered at McGill University as a Graduate Student in the term to which you are applying. McGill student not currently employed at McGill University, must apply via external postings. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. Please note, you must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Candidates who are current McGill employees receiving a salary (not a graduate student stipend) must apply through their already active Workday account. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research are conducted in English, thereby requiring English communication, both verbal and written. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-16 Position End Date: 2027-04-28 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Course-Lecturer----URBP-623-Planning-Studio-2_JR0000082507": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer -  URBP 623 Planning Studio 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Co-instructor position for an Urban Planning Studio. The focus is on urban design and development of a housing project. The course meets on Mondays from 2:35 pm to 3:55 pm and Thursdays from 2:35-5:25 pm from January 7 to April 14. The position requires a knowledge of Montreal housing policy, community organizing and various models of delivering non-profit housing, as well as urban design principles. Salary: $11,824 (3 credits worth) Teaching Qualification Requirements: The candidate must detain an MSc. (or higher) degree in urban planning, urban studies or a related discipline, and should be familiar with Studio-based teaching in an urban planning teaching context. They should have some professional experience in site- or real-estate development (either directly or in a close management or advisory role), ideally in the housing sector and ideally in both private and not-for-profit contexts. Preference given to applicants who have relevant academic teaching experience and publications. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: School of Urban Planning Course Title: Planning Studio 2 Subject Code: URBP 623 Location: Macdonald Harrington Building Schedule: Mondays from 12:35 to 3:55 and Thursdays from 2:35 pm to 5:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistant---GPHL-591---W2027_JR0000082505": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - GPHL 591 - W2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Global and Public Health Course Title: Special Topics in Global and Population Health 1 (Confronting Medical Colonialism) Course Code: GPHL 591 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 68 Position Summary: Must attend all lectures in-person Primary responsibility for 5 quizzes (co-development, management and grading), and 2 written assignments (grading); assist with evaluation of final group presentations Assist with online course management via myCourses (primary responsibility for keeping track of students’ attendance, setting up discussion board, entering grades, etc.) and coordinating occasional guest lectures by Zoom Clarify course content and assignments with students, including via weekly announcements Help prepare and facilitate class discussions, media sharing and group work Respond to email communications from students and share pertinent information or concerns with course supervisor as appropriate; holding office hours to meet students may be requested Uphold academic integrity and communicate concerns with course supervisor Meet regularly (i.e., monthly) with course supervisor Qualifications: PhD candidate preferred, but 2nd year Master's student or senior (3rd/4th year) part-time Master's student very welcome to apply Organized and efficient with good time management skills Demonstrated proficiency in English reading and writing Proficiency in spoken and written French is strongly encouraged Attention to detail regarding all course aspects Resourceful, approachable, and thoughtful, with a commitment to cultural safety & humility Familiar with course materials and assignments, including medical colonialism in settler-colonial contexts and Indigenous communities in Canada Individuals with previous Teaching Assistant experience and a background in social sciences are encouraged to apply How to Apply To be eligible for a Teaching Assistant position you must be registered at McGill University as a Graduate Student in the term to which you are applying. McGill student not currently employed at McGill University, must apply via external postings. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. Please note, you must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Candidates who are current McGill employees receiving a salary (not a graduate student stipend) must apply through their already active Workday account. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research are conducted in English, thereby requiring English communication, both verbal and written. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-16 Position End Date: 2026-11-01 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Course-Lecturer---URBP-645-Social-Research-Methods-1_JR0000082503-1": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - URBP 645 Social Research Methods 1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Selected social research techniques as applied in urban planning, including the art and craft of interviewing; surveys and questionnaires; ethnography, participant observation and narrative analysis; participatory techniques. Salary: $3,941.34 (1 credit course) Tuesdays 8:35 am to 11:35 am January 12, 2027 to February 2, 2027 Teaching Qualification Requirements: PhD degree in Urban Planning and teaching experience Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: School of Urban Planning Course Title: Social Research Methods 1 Subject Code: URBP 645 Location: Macdonald Harrington Building Schedule: Tuesdays from 8:35 am to 11:25 am Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
