    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Research-Associate_JR0000082145": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Department of Mechanical Engineering at McGill University is seeking a Research Associate for the Reprogrammable metamaterials research project under the supervision of Professor Damiano Pasini. This role will contribute to the analysis, design, fabrication, and testing of mechanical metamaterials. This is a unionized position, and the incumbent will be a member of the AMURE (Research Associates and Assistants) bargaining unit. Responsibilities: Analyze and design optimize metamaterial unit cells and lattice architectures with tunable or switchable mechanical properties, including multistable, origami/kirigami-inspired and stimuli-responsive designs. Develop analytical and computational models (e.g., finite element analysis, discrete/reduced-order models) to predict nonlinear mechanical behavior, instabilities and reconfiguration pathways. Fabricate prototypes using additive manufacturing (multi-material 3D printing, laser cutting) and other fabrication techniques. Plan and conduct mechanical experiments (quasi-static and dynamic testing, digital image correlation) to characterize and validate metamaterial performance. Investigate actuation and programming strategies using mechanical, thermal, magnetic or electrical stimuli. Analyze data, interpret results and compare experimental findings with theoretical predictions. Prepare manuscripts for peer-reviewed journals, present at conferences, and contribute to grant proposals and reports. Collaborate with an interdisciplinary team and mentor undergraduate and graduate students involved in the design, fabrication, testing, and analysis of reprogrammable mechanical metamaterials. Qualifications: PhD in Mechanical Engineering or Physics or related fields. At least five (5) years of research experience. Strong background in solid mechanics, multiscale theory, nonlinear elasticity, structural stability and buckling. Proficiency with finite element software (e.g., ABAQUS, COMSOL or ANSYS) and scientific programming (Python or MATLAB). Knowledge of mechanical metamaterials, architected materials or soft/smart materials. Experience with mechanical testing equipment and experimental data analysis. Excellent written and oral communication skills in English. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $35.37 Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Location: Macdonald Eng. Bldg. Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-09 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Raymond-Building/Teaching-Assistant-Positions---Department-of-Plant-Science---Winter-2027_JR0000082418": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Positions - Department of Plant Science - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Teaching Assistant Positions - Department of Plant Science - Winter 2027 These postings are directed to graduate students who will be registered at McGill University in the winter 2027. Applications from others will not be considered. Winter 2027 Application Period: October  19 - November 2 Job start and end dates:  January 5 to April 30, 2027 Teaching Assistants must be available to work the entire contract period. When applying for the positions below, please first consult the up-to-date Winter 2027 McGill class schedule on Minerva to avoid any conflicts. Application for these courses must be completed in Workday ( https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/careers ).  All students should apply as external candidates regardless of current employment status at McGill.   You must be a McGill graduate student to be eligible to apply and must be eligible to work in Canada. A detailed list of positions is available here:  AES TA POSTINGS Estimated Number of Positions: 9 Total Hours of Work per Term: Varies by course; 45-80 hours Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required on campus to perform their tasks in person. Should anything prohibit in-person teaching activities, courses will revert to remote teaching. In the event of such a change, Teaching Assistants will be advised as soon as possible. Teaching Assistants may be required to perform tasks suited to a remote/online teaching environment using a virtual learning platform [e.g., Zoom, Teams, WebEx] and the McGill myCourses learning management system . Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located (Macdonald Campus). McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Application Deadline: November 2, 2026 (11:59 p.m.) Note: This posting will expire at 00:00 a.m. on November 3. You have until 11:59 p.m. on November 2 to submit your application. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacdonaldStewart-Bld/Teaching-Assistant-Positions---School-of-Human-Nutrition---Winter-2027_JR0000082360": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Positions - School of Human Nutrition - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Teaching Assistant Positions - School of Human Nutrition - Winter 2027 These postings are directed to graduate students who will be registered at McGill University in the winter 2027. Applications from others will not be considered. Winter 2027 Application Period: October  19 - November 2 Job start and end dates:  January 5 to April 30, 2027 Teaching Assistants must be available to work the entire contract period. When applying for the positions below, please first consult the up-to-date Winter 2027 McGill class schedule on Minerva to avoid any conflicts. Application for these courses must be completed in Workday ( https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/careers ).  All students should apply as external candidates regardless of current employment status at McGill.   You must be a McGill graduate student to be eligible to apply and must be eligible to work in Canada. A detailed list of positions is available here:  AES TA POSTINGS Estimated Number of Positions: 16 Total Hours of Work per Term: Varies by course; 45-85 HOURS Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required on campus to perform their tasks in person. Should anything prohibit in-person teaching activities, courses will revert to remote teaching. In the event of such a change, Teaching Assistants will be advised as soon as possible. Teaching Assistants may be required to perform tasks suited to a remote/online teaching environment using a virtual learning platform [e.g., Zoom, Teams, WebEx] and the McGill myCourses learning management system . Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located (Macdonald Campus). McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Application Deadline: November 2, 2026 (11:59 p.m.) Note: This posting will expire at 00:00 a.m. on November 3. You have until 11:59 p.m. on November 2 to submit your application. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
