    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistant-DENT-246_JR0000081892": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant DENT 246",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Course Title: Management of Partial Edentulism Course Code: DENT 246 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 48.5 Hiring Unit: Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences Course Title and Course Number: Management of Partial Edentulism DENT 246 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 48.5 hours Job description:  Teaching and Mentorship - TAs should have a strong general knowledge of removable partial dentures (RPDs), including RPD classification, components, surveying, design principles, biomechanics, and clinical and laboratory procedures. - TAs will provide active hands-on support during laboratory sessions, including RPD classification exercises, fabrication of record bases and custom trays, RPD surveying, design of Kennedy Class I–IV RPDs and laboratory prescriptions, and tooth preparation for RPDs. - Duties include demonstrating and reinforcing laboratory techniques, guiding students through exercises, troubleshooting technical difficulties, providing feedback, and ensuring appropriate use of materials and equipment. - TAs will also assist with the evaluation of students’ laboratory work according to the assessment criteria and evaluation sheets provided by the course director.   Qualifications required:   - Hold a DDS, DMD, or foreign equivalent. - Sound knowledge of removable partial dentures and proficiency in the clinical and laboratory techniques required for RPD design and fabrication. - Must be registered in an MSc or PhD program in Dentistry or a related field. Name & Title of Course Supervisor: Dr. Carolina B. Meloto Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-06-01 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Laird-Hall/Administrative-Officer_JR0000081954": {
      "title": "Administrative Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: McGill’s Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences, located on the Macdonald Campus, 35km from downtown Montreal, is looking for an Administrative Officer to provide a high level of organizational, planning, communication and administrative advice to the McGill Academic Lead and the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences’ Academic Lead of the Nkabom Collaborative at McGill University, and to contribute to the efficient operation of the Collaborative in promotion of its priorities and mission. The incumbent will manage information, organization, and development of documents and correspondences, and will function with an exceptional level of accuracy, judgement, independence, confidentiality, and efficiency.  They will be responsible for ensuring the smooth functioning of the Nkabom Collaborative’s administrative affairs.   McGill University, in partnership with the Mastercard Foundation, is embarking on an ambitious collaboration with seven African organizations within the Nkabom Nutrition and Sustainability Agri-Food Collaborative.  This initiative aims to bridge education and workforce gaps by training youth in the nutrition and agri-food sectors, especially in rural communities, to help transform Africa’s agri-food system and empower the next generation of leaders.   Reporting to the Director Administration, Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences (AES), the Administrative Officer is the operational and administrative manager of the Collaborative’s McGill Partner Academic Lead's office and the AES’ Academic Lead’s office; in addition, they will act as a liaison between the Academic Lead’s office and other McGill partners of the Collaborative as well as external organizations within both Canada and Ghana with travel to Ghana a few times a year. The incumbent will develop and maintain administrative standards and practices for the Academic Lead’s office and McGill partners.  The incumbent will also be responsible for general office management ensuring the smooth day-to-day functioning of a complex, multi-stakeholder program spanning Canada and Ghana, maintaining institutional and collaborative records and management of the Collaborative’s capital assets. Primary Responsibilities: ·         Oversees a broad range of operational, administrative, and logistical functions critical to the effective functioning of the unit. Provide administrative advice and management in all activities related to the implementation of the McGill Partner collaborative programs as well as the facilitation of the program deliverables. Develop and implement administrative procedures and internal controls. Establishes financial priorities. Develops and implements budgetary planning, recommends budget allocations and prepares and submits operating budget. Ensures preparation of appropriate reporting and year-end documentation. Acts as advisor regarding University Financial Policies and Procedures, monitors and reports on account spending and exercises financial controls where applicable. Undertakes analysis of budgeting issues, assesses impact to the University/unit, articulate alternatives and recommend solutions. Acts as approver for office financial functions. Administer human resources processes for Collaborative personnel (Postdoctoral Fellows, Visiting Academics etc.), including recruitment, hiring, onboarding, offboarding, and related HR administration. Responsible for organizing Nkabom events hosted by McGill, including the MFCC meetings, stakeholder engagement sessions, and Annual Forum activities. Prepare reports, briefing materials, presentations, communications, and maintain collaborative documentation and web content. Develop and maintain systems for Collaborative records, data collection, document management and information tracking. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Demonstrated knowledge of accounting and financial management. Knowledge of University budgeting policies and fund types.  Ability to effectively communicate and interact with various levels of staff, academics and other stakeholders.  Service oriented with excellent customer service skills and the ability to establish productive working relationships quickly.  Experience organizing complex events, conferences, workshops, or international visits. Proficiency with computerized applications including advanced spreadsheets, databases, word-processing software, Banner FIS, Workday HR business processes and other platforms Excellent analytical and problem solving skills. Ability to exercise sound judgment, discretion, and diplomacy when handling confidential or sensitive information. Ability to anticipate operational challenges and proactively identify solutions. Proven organizational skills, capacity to make decisions independently, ability to multitask, prioritize, and meet deadlines.  Proven ability to develop and improve administrative processes, procedures, and controls. Demonstrated ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative environment while maintaining a high degree of autonomy. Demonstrated intercultural competency and experience working with diverse populations, international partners, and cross-cultural teams. Ability to express oneself clearly and concisely, verbally and in writing, in English; as a minimum, proficiency in spoken French an asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Administration Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-12-23 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Res-Carrefour-Sherb/Associate-Director-Communications--Resident-Admissions-and-Customer-Service_JR0000081983": {
      "title": "Associate Director Communications, Resident Admissions and Customer Service",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Student Housing and Hospitality Services Position Summary: Under the direction of the Senior Director Student Housing and Hospitality Services (SHHS), the Associate Director Communications, Residence Admissions and Customer Service promote and present a consistent and cohesive image of Student Housing and Hospitality Services (SHHS) within McGill University. Is responsible for promoting and maintaining a consistent and cohesive image of SHHS within McGill University. The position leads the support the Unit's and the University's overall mission related to marketing and communications.  Development and implementation of SHHS’s marketing, communications, residence admissions and customer service initiatives in alignment with the Unit’s and University’s mission and objectives. This includes communications services such as public relations, social media, translation, graphic design, audiovisual, photography, web and multimedia, marketing campaigns such as events, advertising, sales and promotional initiatives, and managing daily operations of residence admissions and customer services. The Associate Director oversees marketing efforts across SHHS operations, including self-operated locations and the main food service provider, and directs the development and production of print and promotional materials. services consistent with the University's mission and objectives, including news, public relations, social media, translation, graphic design, audiovisual, photography, web and multimedia. Organize marketing campaigns and events to maximize revenue streams of Student Housing and Food Services. Develop and manage advertising, sales and marketing techniques and practices. Administer and supervise all marketing efforts with SHHS, which includes all self-opt locations and main food service provider to maximize student engagement and generate revenues. Administer, analyze, supervise, manage and direct the activities related to all print material. Provide editorial, production, design, and advertising services of the highest quality to raise SHHS visibility to prospective students and maximize revenues. Keep up to date on new developments and trends in the fields of marketing, communication and nutrition education. Primary Responsibilities: Communication: Develop and implement effective communication of information, policies, procedures, and position statements for SHHS using several channels, including social media, web and other electronic service delivery. Oversee the management of 5 websites for SHHS and social media platforms. Oversee and manage the production of visual communications and graphic design materials for all SHHS subunits, ensuring alignment with McGill’s visual identity standards and brand guidelines. Create and implement marketing and communication plans for outreach events such as Open House, Campus Connect, move-in weekend.  Collaborate with Enrollment Services, Student Services and other university partners to drive the recruitment of prospective resident students. Review, edit and approve SHHS pre-arrival seminars and student-facing communications for incoming students, ensuring accuracy and consistency across all residences. Collaborate with other campus partners such as the Welcome Center, Enrolment Services to review SHHS related information on Campus tours. Oversee the development and implementation of marketing strategies related to residence admissions, oneCard and meal plan promotion, and the publicity of all food service activities/events (McGill a la Carte, Awards and achievements) Collaborate with Student Life and Learning (SLL) and Communications and Institutional Relations (CIR) on media requests related to SHHS and supports the development of timely and appropriate media responses. Participate in various committees. Member of the leadership team and participate in other various committees. Collaborate with PIA or Student Services in the planning, implementation of surveys and producing reports when needed. Manage marketing budget. Event planning – Create McGill community-wide events such as the Fair-Trade Campus of the year celebration, Roddick Roast launch, using multiple McGill News vehicles and social media. Residence Admissions: Manage and oversee the daily operations of the residence admissions office. Oversee the Customer Service Office, which includes a team comprised of a Residence Admissions Supervisor and Customer Service Representatives, ensuring effective delivery of residence admissions and customer service operations Manage admission and retention on two campuses to maintain maximum occupancy levels during the academic year. Manage housing assignments for residence students and assists McGill students with off campus housing needs. Work in close collaboration with Enrollment Services, supporting the University’s goal of attracting the best students to McGill and other goals in the University’s Strategic Enrolment Management Plan Ensure compliance with relevant laws and University policies. Continually work to improve the efficiency and accuracy of the admissions and assigning process. Work with Finance to ensure PCI compliance, efficient processes, and policies are in place regarding charges, refunds, and billing. Supervise the development and maintenance of a student residence admissions waiting list. Develop and update departmental procedures, processes, and guidelines as necessary.   Customer Service: Manage the SHHS Customer Relations Centre, ensuring that all services provided under SHHS (Food Services, Residences, Off Campus Housing) are effective and efficient. Collaborate with relevant stakeholders to implement solutions and improvements. Manage and resolve high-level and sensitive complaints/issues or concerns related to housing and dining from resident students, parents, or alumni and those addressed to the senior director in a timely and professional manner. Provide off campus housing information to all McGill students and assist them with finding suitable and affordable accommodation options. Collaborate with other units in Student Life and Learning, such as Campus Life and Engagement, Student Wellness Hub, Office of the Dean of Students, Office for Students with Disabilities, etc., to ensure a coordinated and holistic approach to student support and success. Network with other campus services and administrative units, external agencies and peer universities as needed, to share best practices, exchange information and collaborate on projects and initiatives related to student housing and hospitality. Represent SHHS on various campus committees and work groups and provide input and feedback on policies, procedures and programs that affect SHHS operations and customers.   Projects: Lead initiatives, such as implementing a centralized ticketing system for housing and food related complaints to enhance student experience, improve service delivery and enable improved monitoring. and Lead the review and process mapping of residence admission practices at both campuses and the development of new Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) to improve operational efficiencies. Review and realign social media practices under SHHS that include MAC and our main food provider to ensure consistent brand representation throughout all channels. Increase our followership, engagement, and feedback from our communities. Oversee and manage the creation of new McGill Student Housing YouTube videos as promotional material for SHHS residences. These videos will showcase the facilities, amenities, food options, testimonials, etc. that we offer to our students. Collaborate with Student Services with the SLL Communication Mapping Project. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-LOG3D - Services Operations - Senior Professional/ Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Director Student Housing and Hospitality Services Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-19 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Accreditation---QI-Administrator_JR0000082182": {
      "title": "Accreditation & QI Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary Reporting to the Accreditation and Quality Improvement Officer and working in close collaboration with administrative and academic leadership, the Accreditation and Quality Improvement Administrator provides administrative and expert support for all School of Medicine accreditation and quality improvement activities. Responsibilities may extend to other projects at the Director’s request. Role and responsibilities: 1. Develops and maintains expert level of knowledge regarding all accreditation standards for Medicine: a. Works in close collaboration with the Accreditation and Q.I. Officer in administering the preparation of comprehensive and high quality self-study reports and progress reports, as necessary. b. Builds and maintains collaborative, productive working relationships with staff and senior academics for the different programs on all accreditation and Q.I. issues. c. Keeps abreast of all process updates and new product developments in Accreditation and Education Quality Improvement d. Liaises with and maintains a partnership with the appropriate accreditation bodies 2. Coordinates and provides administrative support for Accreditation and Education Quality Improvement committees: a. May function as a minute taker b. Manage shared Committee folders with regards to meeting outcomes c. Preparation of agendas d. Room bookings e. Document preparation and printing f. Meeting invitations to Committee members g. Any logistics associated with Committee meeting h. Ensure follow-up of action items in collaboration with the Accreditation and Q.I. Officer 3. Provide administrative support for all accreditation related processes: a. Understands accreditation requirements and timelines. b. Informs administrative personnel on accreditation requirements and in particular, guides the assembly of complex accreditation reports, to best present required materials completely and accurately. c. Develops, applies and implements administrative procedures and/or systems to ensure that data required for all quality assurance documents (e.g., annual reports, cyclical reviews, and accreditation reports) is systematically collected in a timely manner and ensures activities meet or exceed established quality requirements. d. Validates accreditation requirements, investigates data sources for required data and identifies sources. e. In collaboration with the Accreditation and Q.I. Officer, determines the best way to present data clearly to meet requirements. f. Identifies and flags potential threats to accreditation compliance to Accreditation and Q.I. g. Communicates with and provides support to administrative personnel to gather the information and documents required by faculty members. h. Verifies the completeness of reports and communications submitted i. Escalates administrative issues j. Coordinates printing and/or electronic submission of self-study report, to ensure readiness by deadline date. k. Administers onsite visit schedules, confirms and sends reminders for interviews to invited parties. Makes arrangements for onsite team’s travel, accommodations, and meals, as required. Specifically for Onsite Reviews: a. Administers onsite schedules b. Sends calendar invitations and confirms attendance c. Makes arrangements for onsite team’s travel/accommodation/meals, etc. l. Manages document preparation a. Formatting and stylistic editing b. Creating appendices and tables of contents c. First-round proof-reading d. Checks for completeness of documents and forms e. Provide administrative support for Accreditation committees m. Provide administrative support to Accreditation and Quality Improvement Officer as needed 4. Provide project management support for all accreditation related outcomes: a. Develops and implements administrative procedures to track and monitor the accreditation and quality improvement activities b. Develops and implements administrative documents to help monitor and strengthen the quality assurance and quality improvement processes 5. Coordinate and provide administrative support for the different documentation databases used for accreditation documentation 6. Provide educational and administrative support for accreditation related Q.I. activities: a. Designs and implements guides (checklist, timeline guides, etc.) b. Designs and implements toolkits for Program Directors c. Designs and implements report templates (surveyor checklist, internal review report, feedback forms, survey questionnaire, transmittal letters, etc.) d. Establishes ongoing coaching relationship with administrative staff on accreditation related issues e. Develops and implements training sessions for Program Administrators f. In collaboration with Accreditation and Q.I Officer and Assistant Deans, analyzes knowledge/policy gaps and propose solutions g. Works in collaboration with Officer, Assistant Deans and other stakeholders to define new accreditation requirements h. Prepares presentations as needed i. Organizes and prepares formal conferences and special events. 7. Coordinate and provide administrative support for New site accreditation: a. Guides programs through application process for new programs b. Guides programs through process for site visits Participates in special projects as required. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong knowledge of accreditation standards, quality assurance, continuous improvement processes, and medical education program accreditation. Experience supporting medical education programs, including accreditation reviews, site visits, and compliance-related activities. Experience working closely with senior leaders, faculty members, and administrators within the School of Medicine. Experience supporting multiple functional areas and managing competing priorities in a complex academic environment. Knowledge of School of Medicine and/or Postgraduate Medical Education (PGME) policies, processes, and governance structures. Excellent organizational and project coordination skills with the ability to manage multiple projects, deadlines, and priorities. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills with the ability to interpret data, identify risks, and recommend solutions. Excellent written communication skills with experience preparing, editing, and reviewing complex reports, submissions, and correspondence. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite and electronic document management systems. Knowledge of accreditation processes for the Royal College. Knowledge of School of Medicine platforms like one45 considered an asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Administration Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-24 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Laird-Hall/Cleaner--MUNACA-_JR0000082213": {
      "title": "Cleaner (MUNACA)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Student Housing & Hospitality Services Macdonald Campus Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, performs cleaning and housekeeping duties. Cleans and washes floors, walls, furnishings, fixtures, etc. Changes linens. Washes blankets, towels, etc. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Mops, sweeps and washes staircases and floors. Strips, waxes and polishes floors. Washes walls and doors. Washes or cleans furnishings and fixtures such as interior and exterior window panes, partitions, window sills and swinging shutters, blinds, elevators, picture frames, extinguishers, etc. cleans refrigerators, freezers, counters, sinks, etc. Cleans and washes washrooms; washes fixtures and accessories such as sinks, urinals, toilets, mirrors, garbage cans, etc. Unclogs sinks and toilets with appropriate products and plungers. Restocks hand soaps, hand towels, toilet paper, etc. As required, cleans, washes and wipes partitions in shower stalls. Vacuums and shampoos carpets as required. Empties and cleans ashtrays; empties and periodically cleans waste paper baskets. Picks up debris, cleans and washes rooms that have been damaged. Cleans areas affected by water damage. Performs heavy cleaning such as spring-cleaning and maintenance duties as required. Handles, carries and uses equipment, tools and materials required to perform duties; maintains tools and equipment and ensures cleanliness of work area. Informs immediate supervisor any regularities or defects; takes measures to correct the situation. Uses equipment such as vacuum cleaners, floor polishers, ladder, mops, brooms, cleaning accessories, hammers, snow shovel, etc. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Able to carry out housekeeping tasks in the assigned areas, such as cleaning administrative offices, disinfecting washrooms, laundry rooms, kitchens, etc. with the use of equipment such as vacuum cleaners, floor polishers, ladders, mops, brooms, cleaning accessories, linen cart, etc. Demonstrated ability to carry out general repairs and perform moderately to strenuous physical tasks such as climbing ladders, washing walls, lifting garbage cans and carrying to exterior dumpster, moving furniture, pushing/pulling equipment, shoveling entrances. Responsible for informing the Cleaning Service Coordinator of any irregularities or defects; takes measures to correct the situation. Ability to clearly transmit and receive instructions. Knowledge of French and English. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Additional Information: Work schedule is Monday to Friday; 6h30 - 14h30. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level A) $21.99 - $27.24 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Supervisor: Accommodations Supervisor (ER) Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-27 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Technical-Specialist-D--Recording-Assistant---DDMAL-_JR0000082207": {
      "title": "Technical Specialist D (Recording Assistant - DDMAL)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Schulich School of Music - Department of Music Research - DDMAL Position Summary: Assist the recording engineer and research team in preparing and running recording sessions for the LinkedMusic research project. Typical Functions : Help with equipment setup and teardown, including stands, risers and cables; Assist with microphone placement for choir and ensemble sessions; Set up monitoring and perform basic equipment checks and troubleshooting; Monitor levels and keep a session log; Back up and transfer files and label recordings and metadata following project conventions; Transport and store equipment; Support participants and performers during sessions. Qualifying skills and abilities : Hands-on experience with microphones, audio interfaces and a DAW; Experience with live or ensemble recording, ideally choirs or vocal groups; Familiarity with McGill's on-campus recording spaces and equipment; Good organization, reliability and documentation habits; Good communication and teamwork; Ability to follow procedures and to lift and carry equipment; Availability for sessions scheduled around ensemble rehearsals. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 3 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE D) $18.92 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-02 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Wellness-Ambassador_JR0000082209-1": {
      "title": "Wellness Ambassador",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Desautels Faculty of Management   Position Summary:  Under the direction of Desautels’ Resilience and Wellness Associate; and under the direct supervision of the Wellness Ambassador Program Coordinator, the Wellness Ambassador may perform the listed tasks: • Participate in current wellness activities and contribute to the creation of new wellness initiatives within the McGill Desautels Faculty of Management. • Act as a resource for planning, promoting, scheduling, setting up, hosting, and following up on wellness related events as needed. • Create content for the McGill Desautels Wellness Instagram account. • Engage in public speaking to promote wellness resources on campus. • Assist the Resilience and Wellness (RAW) committee members with tasks such as taking minutes, organizing events, and preparing documents and reports as needed. • Liaise with RAW committee members to identify key wellness related needs within the faculty, gather materials related to wellness education, and produce promotional materials. • Acts as a liaison between the unit and internal or external parties, such as fellow McGill students, to provide navigation assistance or information regarding wellness resources. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by the Wellness Ambassador Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Applicant must be passionate about health promotion and student wellness. Great verbal communication skills and interpersonal skills. Demonstrated experience with group work and event organization. Ability to receive and relay information with accuracy. Ability to work both autonomously and as part of a team. Ability and/or willingness to share wellness resources through class presentations and other public speaking opportunities. Ability to manage the Desautels Instagram account, including creating posts and stories. Experience in the production of posters and promotional material with a proven ability to work with Canva or similar applications is an asset. Advanced English, spoken and written, is a requirement. Must be a currently registered undergraduate student in the Desautels Faculty of Management. Current Year 0 and Year 1 students are preferred. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $19.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 3 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2027-01-01 Position End Date: 2027-05-31 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacdonaldStewart-Bld/Assistant-Professor--CAS--in-Sustainable-Agri-Food-Systems_JR0000081215": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (CAS) in Sustainable Agri-Food Systems",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description   The Department of Food Science and Agricultural Chemistry in the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences of McGill University invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position at the rank of Assistant Professor (teaching) in the field of Sustainable Agri-food Systems . This will be a Contract Academic Staff position for three years and does not confer eligibility for tenure.   McGill’s Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences is located on the Macdonald Campus, 35 km from downtown Montreal. The Campus comprises 650 hectares of farm and forested lands, commercial and research animal facilities, experimental field stations, well-equipped analytical laboratories and state-of-the-art student learning facilities. McGill field stations include the Bellairs Institute in Barbados, the McGill Arctic Research Station on Axel Heiberg Island and the McGill Subarctic Research Station in northern Quebec, as well as the Morgan Arboretum, the Gault Reserve and the Molson Reserve. Additional information concerning McGill University and its Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences can be found on their respective web sites ( www.mcgill.ca , www.mcgill.ca/macdonald ).   McGill University, in partnership with the MasterCard Foundation, is embarking on an ambitious collaboration with seven African organizations within the Nkabom Nutrition and Sustainable Agri-Food Collaborative. This initiative aims to bridge education and workforce gaps by training youth in the nutrition and agri-food sectors, especially in rural communities, to help transform Africa’s agri-food system and empower the next generation of leaders. The collaborative is anchored in three key strategic outcomes: (i) the development of experiential learning opportunities that promote climate-smart, traditional, and nutrition-sensitive agri-food practices through community-based research and innovation; (ii) expanded access to inclusive and flexible learning pathways for diverse youth populations, including women, refugees, internally displaced persons, and persons with disabilities; and (iii) the strengthening of education ecosystems with the policies, networks, skills, and leadership required to support youth-driven entrepreneurship and foster inclusive economic growth within the agri-food sector. Funding for the hiring of this innovative partnership brings together leading Ghanaian institutions and McGill University to transform the nutrition–agri-food ecosystem and create dignified and impactful employment opportunities for young people, particularly marginalized persons and communities, including women. As such, the Nkabom Collaborative represents a key international initiative for McGill, with the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences (AES) playing a central role in coordinating and delivering its academic programs. The objectives of the Nkabom Collaborative closely align with the teaching and research strengths of the Department of Food Science and Agricultural Chemistry, making it well positioned to contribute meaningfully to the initiative’s implementation and impact. This recruitment aligns with Québec’s Politique bioalimentaire 2025–2035 , which emphasizes increasing agricultural productivity and food sovereignty, improving nutritional quality, protecting agricultural land, reducing greenhouse gas emissions, and promoting sustainable development.   Funding for hiring a Contract Academic Staff member at the rank of Assistant Professor (Teaching) for three years has been allocated in the said Nkabom Collaborative initiative funding.  Job Duties Overall, this position is critical to ensuring the effective delivery, academic integration, and long-term impact of McGill’s engagement in the Nkabom Collaborative. The successful candidate’s primary responsibilities will include, but are not limited to, the following: · Building and managing the above, working collaboratively with a diverse network of local and external partners; · Participate in regular meetings at McGill University and key partner institutions; · Developing, teaching, and leading courses in sustainable and equitable agri-food systems and participating in the development and support of degree programs within the partner collaborating institutions.   The successful candidate’s management duties will include: - Overseeing the implementation of the collaborative program with universities in Ghana and related stakeholders; - Responsible for deliverables and outcomes; - Oversee the coordination and management of FAES based training of HQPs involved in this collaborative; - Prepare and communicate FAES quarterly reports to the Nkabom secretariate; - Travel to Ghana to fulfill some of the job duties. The successful candidate will also be expected to be actively involved in all aspects of McGill’s academic mission (i.e., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees, where applicable).   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected.   Qualifications and Education Requirements Have attained a Ph.D. degree; Possess relevant expertise in areas of sustainable agri-food processing, food product development, food security, and food science.  Expertise in human nutrition and experience in program evaluation will be counted as additional asset; Have a track record of high-quality teaching, inclusive pedagogy, and effective student supervision experience in agri-food innovation and nutritional food science; Possess relevant training, research, or work experience in Low- and Middle-Income Countries (LMICs) with preference given to those with experience in Africa and Ghana; Have a proven track record of working in equitable and diverse partnerships; Fluency in English is essential; a working knowledge of French, or a commitment to acquiring it within a few years of appointment is preferred. Position Details   Job Type:   Contract Academic Staff Rank:   Assistant Professor (non-tenured, ranked contract academic staff) Job Status:   Full-time Length of Appointment:   Three (3) year term Salary:   Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience Application Procedure Posting Period: Please submit your application within 30 days of the publication of this advertisement. Required Documents: The following supporting documents are required in one PDF : Cover letter summarizing relevant educational background and work experience (maximum two pages); Curriculum vitae; Statement of teaching philosophy, statement of applied research and interest, including experience in the African context and a demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion (maximum 2 pages); Names, titles, and contact information of three referees. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. Inquiries can be addressed to Dr. Benjamin Simpson, Search Committee Chair: Benjamin.simpson@mcgill.ca Depending on your nationality, it may be necessary for you to have a valid Canadian Visa to travel should you be invited to a Campus visit. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/French-Activities-Facilitator_JR0000082172": {
      "title": "French Activities Facilitator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-13\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of the Associate Provost, Rayonnement du français  Position Summary: The Rayonnement du français team creates informal, social, cultural and experiential opportunities to live and practise French, for students and members of the McGill community. These activities are open to anyone who wants to practise French, socialize in French, or simply live more of their McGill experience in French, whatever their level or relationship to the language.  Successful candidates will join a team of student facilitators who bring this programming to life, from conversation circles and social activities on campus to cultural events and outings in Montreal and beyond.  The goal is to give participants memorable experiences in French throughout their time at McGill, help them build connections, and introduce them to the diversity of French-speaking cultures in a friendly and engaging setting.  Main Responsibilities:   Lead social activities in French and help build a welcoming, lively community around the French language.  Facilitate conversation circles and group activities that encourage exchange and French practice.  Welcome participants and create a relaxed, friendly atmosphere where everyone feels comfortable taking part.  Encourage conversation and adapt facilitation to participants' profiles, interests and levels of French.  Lead or co-lead cultural activities, events and outings in Montreal.  Suggest ideas and help prepare activities, both as part of a team and independently.  Take part, as needed, in welcome, orientation and promotional activities related to Rayonnement du français initiatives.  Help promote activities and, as needed, create content for Rayonnement du français communication channels and social media.  Help with setup and logistics for activities and events.  Perform other related duties.     Workload:   The schedule is flexible, from 5 to 10 hours per week, with the possibility of additional hours depending on the successful candidate's availability and the needs of the program. Schedules are set around each facilitator's classes and availability. The successful candidate must be able to be present on campus and take part in the activities and outings planned as part of the programming.  Required Skills and Qualifications:   Be enrolled at McGill as an undergraduate or graduate student and in good academic standing.  Have a strong enough command of French to comfortably lead conversations and activities in French.  Be personable, punctual and highly conscientious.  Show creativity, enthusiasm and kindness.  Know how to foster exchange and encourage participation, including from quieter participants.  Be comfortable interacting with people from a range of linguistic and cultural backgrounds.  Be flexible, independent and resourceful.    Assets (not required):   Experience leading activities or groups.  Knowledge of the field of language learning.  Experience in teaching, tutoring or mentoring.  Experience in communications or content creation.    Minimum Education and Experience:   High School Diploma 2 years of related experience Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $25.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-01 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-29 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
