    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Teaching-Assistants-Winter-2027_JR0000082126": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistants Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Bioengineering Department Winter 2027 Teaching Assistants   Hiring Unit:   Department of Bioengineering, Faculty of Engineering   Estimated Number of Positions: 37    Position Summary: A Teaching Assistant is a Graduate Student employed to assist a Course Supervisor. •    Assist Professor in preparing, grading, and reviewing HW sets & other assignments, quizzes, exams.  •    Prepare, conduct, and lead tutorials •    Reading course materials •    Communication with students regarding MyCourses   Course code, course title, hours and number of positions BIEN 203, Introduction to Statistics and Data Science, 180 hours, 2 positions BIEN 221/220, Introduction to Mechanics for Bioengineers, 180 hours, 4 positions BIEN 267, Bioanalytical Methods in Bioengineering, 180 hours, 7 positions BIEN 280, Thermodynamics in Bioengineering, 180 hours, 1 position BIEN 320, Molecular, Cellular and Tissue Biomechanics, 180 hours, 1 position BIEN 330, Tissue Engineering and Regenerative Medicine, 90 hours, 1 position BIEN 340, Transport Phenomena in Biological Systems 2, 180 hours, 1 position BIEN 360, Physical Chemistry for Engineers, 150 hours, 1 position BIEN 361, Materials for Bioengineering, 160 hours, 1 position BIEN 420, Biodevices Design for Diagnostics and Screening, 180 hours, 3 positions BIEN 440, Information Storage and Processing in Biological Systems, 85 hours, 1 position BIEN 462, Engineering Principles in Physiological Systems, 85 hours, 1 position BIEN 470, Bioengineering Project Design, 35 hours, 1 position BIEN 514, Fundamentals and Rheology of Biological Fluids, 160 hours, 1 position BIEN 530, Imaging and Bioanalytical Instrumentation, 170 hours, 1 position BIEN 535, Electron Microscopy and 3D Imaging for Biological Materials, 60 hours, 2 positions BIEN 545, Diagnostic Devices at the Point-of-Care, 160 hours, 1 position BIEN 580, Synthetic Biology, 80 hours, 1 position and 120 hours, 1 position BIEN 590, Cell Culture Engineering, 150 hours, 1 position BIEN 602, RNA technology for biomanufacturing, 180 hours, 1 position BIEN 680, Bioprocessing of Vaccines, 180 hours, 1 position BIEN 685, Gene and Cell Therapy Viral Vectors Biomanufacturing, 180 hours, 1 position BBME 600 Seminar series, 45 hours, 1 position   Qualifications: •    Applicants must currently be students enrolled (Winter 2027) in a graduate program at McGill University. Priority will be given to students currently in the priority pool, and graduate students.  •    Applicants must be familiar with the course content (please consult Bioengineering (B.Eng.) (142 credits) | Course Catalogue - McGill University )  •     Proficiency in English is required •     Check course specific TA qualifications on this page ( Teaching Assistant, Grader, and Course Assistant Positions | Department of Bioengineering - McGill University )    Hourly Salary: $38.46   Position Start Date: 2027-01-04   Position End Date: 2027-4- 24 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-550/Communication---Publication-Coordinator_JR0000081613": {
      "title": "Communication & Publication Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Please note that this is a part-time contractual position. Position Summary: Under the direction of the Senior Office Coordinator, the incumbent provides administrative and operational support to the Centre for Research on Children and Families (CRCF), in the Faculty of Arts, including the coordination of communications, organization of events, records, information systems, and technology resources to support the Centre's activities and operations. Major Duties and Responsibilities: . Maintain and regularly update the CRCF website and Facebook page, ensuring content is accurate, current, and aligned with CRCF activities and communications objectives Coordinate the administrative and logistical aspects of CRCF events, including symposiums, conferences, seminars, and meetings; arrange travel accommodations for participants and stakeholders; and process related expense reimbursements in accordance with University policies and procedures. Coordinate the organization, maintenance, and consolidation of CRCF's digital filing systems, including SharePoint, OneDrive, and shared network drives, to ensure effective information management, accessibility, and operational efficiency. Serve as a resource person for software applications, shared network spaces, electronic records management, user permissions, and related systems and processes. Administer the CRCF Listserv and membership records, including maintaining distribution lists, updating member information, distributing communications and documentation, and tracking membership-related data. Gather information and provide administrative support for the preparation of annual reports and other Centre publications. Coordinate the Centre's calendar, room bookings, research seminars, board meetings, and Annual General Meeting (AGM), ensuring effective scheduling, resource allocation, and logistical support. Liaise with technical support personnel to coordinate technology resources, workspace requirements, and the resolution of hardware and software issues. Coordinate the procurement, deployment, and inventory management of computer equipment and technology resources, including loaner laptops and related hardware. Coordinate physical space allocation and facilities-related requirements to support the effective operation of the Centre. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Strong organizational, prioritization, and time management skills, with the ability to manage multiple deadlines and competing priorities. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with a strong client-service orientation. Experience coordinating meetings, events, travel arrangements, and administrative logistics. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, SharePoint, OneDrive, and electronic records management systems. Experience maintaining websites, mailing lists, records, and stakeholder communications. Strong attention to detail and ability to maintain accurate documentation, memberships, and reports. Ability to provide administrative and technical support related to software, systems, and technology resources. Familiarity with Workday and experience processing expense reimbursements is an asset. Ability to work independently and collaboratively while exercising sound judgment and initiative. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 (advanced) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Documentation and Communications 2 Years Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level D) $26.18 - $32.46 Hours per Week: 13.5 (Part time) Supervisor: Senior Office Coordinator Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-08 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Assistant-Professor-of-Cello_JR0000081305": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Cello",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-09\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Schulich School of Music of McGill University is Canada’s leading institution for the professional training of performers and composers, and the humanities-based and scientific research in music and sound. We seek individuals with an international profile in music performance and research who will maintain a high level of professional activity and are able to teach and supervise students in all university degree and diploma programs through to the doctoral level. Montreal offers a vibrant multicultural living environment and is consistently rated among the best student cities in the world.   The Department of Performance invites applications for a tenure-track position in Cello at the rank of Assistant Professor, for an initial three-year appointment beginning August 1, 2027 . Appointment at the rank of Associate Professor may be considered for an exceptional candidate.   Job Duties: Academic responsibilities include teaching, research, and service to the University. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active international profile with a high level of professional activity, while teaching and supervising students at the undergraduate, graduate, and diploma levels, including those preparing for professional careers. Teaching responsibilities will include applied instruction and chamber music coaching and may also encompass courses and seminars related to the candidate’s area of expertise. Service responsibilities might include participation in departmental and School committees, as well as contributions to curriculum development and program enhancement. The successful candidate must demonstrate the ability to develop and sustain a level of artistic activity and/or research consistent with the University's expectations for tenure.  Qualifications and Education Requirements: A doctorate or equivalent professional experience, together with a distinguished international reputation as a performer, is required. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in teaching students at the highest levels of performance and scholarship, broad knowledge of the cello repertoire, and a strong commitment to fostering an inclusive, equitable, and supportive learning environment. The candidate will also contribute to student recruitment and outreach initiatives that engage and inspire emerging musicians. Exceptional leadership, organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills are essential, as is the ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues, students, and the broader musical community. Faculty/Department/Unit: Schulich School of Music / Department of Music Performance Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary and rank commensurate with qualifications and experience. The position is subject to final budgetary approval. Required Documents: Applicants should submit the following items using McGill’s platform, Workday (see https://www.mcgill.ca/music/about-us/employment for instructions and links to apply). Further inquiries may be addressed to Prof. Danielle Gaudry, Chair, Department of Performance search.musicperformance@mcgill.ca .   a cover letter outlining the candidate’s qualifications and suitability for the position a detailed curriculum vitae a link to a video recording of a performance demonstrating artistic excellence and containing at least two contrasting selections the names and contact information of three referees   Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, applications should be received by November 13, 2026 . Please note that applicants are not required to submit a teaching video with the initial application. Selected candidates may be invited to participate in a virtual interview. Finalists will be invited to demonstrate their teaching and artistic expertise by teaching a lesson and/or masterclass, coaching a chamber ensemble, presenting a short recital, and meeting with members of the Search Committee. All internal applicants (current, active employees only), please find McGill’s application manual found here: * English: https://general-knowledgebase.mcgill.ca/spaces/SHRKB/pages/54632206/How+to+Apply+for+a+Job+for+Internal+Candidates * French: https://general-knowledgebase.mcgill.ca/spaces/SHRKB/pages/54633556/Comment+postuler+%C3%A0+un+emploi+pour+les+candidats+internes All external applicants (including former employees), please find McGill’s application manual found here (English only): https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/english_applying_as_an_external_candidate_one-pager_1.pdf Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, this  email . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
